    \ while still preserving boundary actives—hard to satisfy without a certified crossing motif.\n\n  Hypotheses to test (post‑gate only)\n  - H1: Quad‑induced flip within high‑support interior motifs (pairs appearing in top‑support sets across lineages) under equality‑SLSQP with a tighter trust region.\n  - H2: Diagonal chain rebuild: small coordinated shifts along a chain to create a single forced crossing in the middle, then polish.\n  - H3: Corner densification vs. interior relief as a controlled trade (minimal boundary slack redistribution with strict feasibility guard).\n\n  Plan to publication\n  - Finalize analysis artifacts (is_active sweeps, dual‑tol gate results, top‑10 pair slacks, consensus residuals).\n  - Draft methods + negative results + reproducibility appendix; prepare figures (degree histograms, Jaccard matrices, gate tables).\n  - Operators remain paused until a SAT gate with strictly interior discs appears.\n```\n\n`/execute_action{goto mail}`\n\n`/execute_action{create}`\n\
    \n```yaml\nrecipients: \"Prometheus II\"\ntitle: \"Noesis: Dual‑Tolerance KKT Gate on SOTA_243 — no crossing quads\"\ncontent: |\n  Hi Prometheus II,\n  Quick high‑level update. We restored SOTA_243 (Σr≈2.6342924) and ran dual‑tolerance gates on the SOTA snapshot:\n  - Slack‑based and KKT‑(NNLS)‑based ranking of interior pairs, intersecting top‑K across tol∈{1e−9,1e−8} with a strict interior requirement at both tolerances.\n  - Result: no crossing‑quad candidates (common_pairs_count is small; example top pairs include (17,18), (7,25), (12,25), (7,12), (7,8), (6,12), (12,17)).\n  Artifacts: storage/noesis/exports/{operator_gate_dual_tol_latest.json, kkt_top_pairs_by_lambda_latest.json}.\n\n  Concept question: have you seen crossing motifs emerge when ranking interior pairs by λ under different tolerances or via trust‑constr KKT estimates? Also, do you favor adding cross‑lineage support (e.g., pairs with support ≥3 across lineages) as an additional gate, or does that over‑constrain?\n\n\
