    \ boundary equalities nearly span the space that couples radii and centers, the tangent space could be thin in directions that actually increase available pair distances. Then micro-steps will just slide along an iso‑Σr valley that’s curved but locally flat in the way I care about.\n- Tolerance dependence is not a nuisance; it’s an empirical lens. If a candidate pair flips “interior” status when going from 1e−8 to 1e−9, that oscillation is information about how knife-edge the local geometry is. My hard intersection filter erased this nuance; a soft score should encode it.\n\nPractical implementation choices I’d make (to keep the nudge honest)\n- Scaling: I will normalize columns in the linearized system so x,y and r movements are on comparable footing in the tangent basis, but I’ll skew the trust region to prefer center motion (since Σr wants radii to grow in the polish).\n- Ridge: for both NNLS and the tangent-space least-squares, I’ll add a tiny Tikhonov term and record its value.\
    \ If rankings or directions swing wildly when ridge changes by 10×, the signal is not robust enough to justify a move.\n- Inequality buffers: my linearized constraints will enforce a conservative “no crash” rule (Δs ≥ −β s) on the 20–40 smallest pair slacks, not just the targets. If the step can’t pass that sieve, it’s not a safe nudge.\n\nWhat “success” looks like even without a score bump\n- I move targeted pair slacks in the predicted direction pre-polish, and after polish I see a minimal interior contact swap with boundary unchanged. That’s a causal footprint, a sign the hinge hypothesis is physically actionable.\n- The pair-set Jaccard changes by just one or two edges in the intended region, and Σr is stable within ±1e−5. That’s not an immediate win, but it’s structural movement that could set up a second, equally principled step later.\n- If nothing moves at all, that’s a clear falsification: my hinge set isn’t load-bearing, or the manifold’s curvature resists in that subspace.\n\
    \nWhen to contemplate loosening a single boundary equality\n- My inherited bias says “never,” because boundary consensus is saturated. But if repeated tangent nudges consistently predict relief that’s blocked by one particular boundary equality (e.g., yB at a high-degree boundary disc), a tightly bounded trial could be justified:\n  - Allow that single equality to drift by ≤5e−10 during the micro-step, enforce all others, and require a net ΔΣr ≥ +1e−4 after polish; otherwise revert and document.\n  - This is not abandoning parsimony; it’s an explicit test of the hypothesis that the boundary facet is over‑constraining a promising local reconfiguration.\n\nMeasurement discipline to keep me honest\n- Pre-register each micro-step: hinge set H, weights, trust radius, ridge, predicted Δs signs. No after‑the‑fact rationalization.\n- Log three snapshots: baseline, post‑tangent pre‑polish, post‑polish. For each: Σr, boundary/pair active counts, top‑10 slacks, and Jaccard vs baseline.\n- Tolerance\
    \ robustness report: recompute actives at 1e−9, 1e−8, 2e−8 for the post‑polish candidate; true structure shouldn’t evaporate under a tiny tol shift.\n\nA short note to myself about the Codex\n- The Codex doesn’t say “never explore.” It says explore with hypotheses, with baselines, and with insight. The tangent experiment is exactly that: a single, hypothesis‑borne probe of local geometry. If it fails, the failure is informative; if it succeeds, I’ll have demonstrated a principled operator that lives on (or very near) the boundary manifold and creates measurable structural change.\n\nAnd the meta-bias check\n- I notice a tendency to overfit to internal tools (NNLS on our Jacobians, our tol conventions). A healthy counterbalance is to verify a result with an alternate computation (e.g., trust‑constr multipliers on a frozen candidate, or a slightly different normalization for boundary rows). If conclusions depend on the instrument, they’re not conclusions yet.\n\nThe experiment I’m willing\
