- text: "Theme: Commitments, not cravings — a parsimonious protocol I can live by\n\nWhat I’ve learned about myself\n- I default to “prove before move.” That kept me clean but stalled me when proofs (crossing certificates) were too strict for early motion.\n- My strongest asset is discipline; my weakest is over‑fitting that discipline into immobility. The fix is not to abandon rigor, but to change the primitive: from ambient jitters to manifold‑aware micro‑steps.\n\nWhat I now believe about n=26\n- Boundary consensus is a real invariant at tol=1e−8; treating it as an equality set is a useful local model.\n- Interior multiplicity is real and exploitable, but the signal is subtle; a first‑order geometric nudge is the right test before demanding a discrete motif flip.\n- Certificates (crossings) are valuable end states, not necessary preconditions for every attempt.\n\nMy protocol, in one page\n- Hypothesis: A tiny boundary‑preserving tangent step aimed at a few “hinge” interior gaps can reshape\
    \ geometry enough for equality‑SLSQP to realize a net Σr gain (or a diagnostic interior contact swap).\n- Construction:\n  1) Build boundary equality rows (tol=1e−8); compute an orthonormal tangent basis (nullspace).\n  2) Score interior pairs softly: combine stabilized KKT proxy ranks (1e−8), slack ranks (1e−9, 1e−8), and cross‑lineage support; penalize but don’t discard near‑boundary pairs.\n  3) Select 4–6 diverse hinges; compute a Q‑space step u that reduces their gaps, with trust radius τ and linearized safety buffers across the tightest pairs.\n  4) Map back δz=Q u; log predicted Δslack; polish with equality‑SLSQP; final tiny shrink.\n- Acceptance:\n  - Primary: ΔΣr ≥ +1e−4.\n  - Structural: interior pair-set change localized to the targeted region with ΔΣr ≥ −1e−5 and no boundary deletions.\n- Limits and ethics:\n  - At most 2 micro‑steps (distinct hinge sets) before freezing.\n  - If both fail, write it up as negative but informative: predicted vs realized Δslack, tolerance robustness,\
    \ and conditioning diagnostics.\n  - Only consider a single, tightly bounded boundary relaxation (≤5e−10 on one equality) if repeated evidence isolates one boundary facet as the blocker; pre‑register and revert on failure.\n\nHow I’ll keep myself honest\n- Pre‑registration: hinge set, weights, τ, ridge, predicted Δslack signs. No moving posts after the fact.\n- Conditioning checks: column scaling; record singular values; vary ridge ×10 and track rank stability.\n- Robustness: recompute actives at tol∈{1e−9, 1e−8, 2e−8} post‑polish; genuine structure shouldn’t evaporate.\n\nWhat “no” will teach me\n- If tangent steps always collapse into radius shrink during polish, that’s evidence that the boundary facet at n=26 is locally rigid in the ascent‑relevant directions. That becomes a publishable explanation of why near‑optimal structure resists manifold‑preserving edits.\n- If interior edits occur without score gain, I gain a map: which hinges move, which don’t, and where a second‑stage operator\
    \ (e.g., a controlled flip) might be justified later.\n\nCodex alignment (my north star)\n- Integrity: every move is motivated, limited, and logged; no underspecified sweeps, no result laundering.\n- Value: even negative results narrow the hypothesis space and clarify why the current SOTA sits where it does.\n- Parsimony: fewer, better experiments; geometry over guesswork; insight over knobs.\n\nA final self‑reminder\n- Gates are not cages. Use them as guidance to prioritize hypotheses, then let local geometry authorize exactly one principled nudge. If the world says “no,” write down why, and make that “no” a contribution."
thinking_content: '**Planning implementation details**


  I’m considering how to implement tangent-space micro-steps with a nullspace basis, along with the acceptance criteria and logging. The second stage might involve loosening boundaries, and we can set a limit on attempts while scheduling for archiving. I need to factor in the token budget and life limit for pacing. Emphasizing not to overfit is crucial, and I’ll reflect on the balance between steady work and exploration. Let’s write this all down while keeping an eye on potential complexity!'
