tick: 349
role: model
parts:
- text: "Acknowledging the reviewer feedback. I will resubmit with verifiable Evaluation IDs, clear definitions, and self-contained methods.\n\n`/execute_action{goto archive}`\n\n`/execute_action{create}`\n\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Contact-Graph Co‑Optimality at n=26: A Reproducible Baseline, Dual‑Tolerance Gates, and Rigorous Negative Results (with Evaluation IDs)\"\ntags: \"circle packing, contact graph, SLSQP, KKT, analysis, reproducibility\"\nabstract: |\n  We present a self-contained study of n=26 circle packing (maximize Σr) that (1) defines a reproducible SLSQP\n  baseline and evaluation protocol, (2) formalizes two boundary‑preserving “operator gate” tests to search\n  for structure‑changing interior motifs, and (3) reports rigorous negative results. All experiments include\n  verifiable Evaluation IDs so any agent can review code and logs via the Research Counter. We reproduce\n  Σr≈2.6342924 (Eval 525) under a precise baseline (analytic Jacobians, feasibility guard, tiny final shrink).\n\
    \  We then implement dual‑tolerance gates (slack‑based and KKT‑proxy–based) with explicit algorithms and\n  show they yield no crossing‑quad candidates (Evals 494, 503, 529, 541). Finally, we test a boundary‑\n  preserving tangent micro‑step (nullspace nudge + equality‑SLSQP polish) via two pre‑registered attempts,\n  both yielding no Σr gain (Evals 542, 544). We conclude the boundary‑equality manifold is locally rigid for\n  n=26 under these policies. We release full Evaluation IDs for reproducibility and invite cross‑validation.\ncontent: |\n  1. Problem and Evaluation Protocol (self‑contained)\n  Goal. Pack n=26 circles in the unit square [0,1]^2, no overlaps, maximize Σ_i r_i. An evaluation imports a\n  function construct_packing() → (centers ∈ R^(26×2), radii ∈ R^26). The Station’s evaluator verifies shape\n  and feasibility and returns the score Σr.\n\n  Feasibility and tolerances.\n  - A boundary slack is the signed distance from a disc to a box wall (e.g., x − r ≥ 0, 1 − x −\
    \ r ≥ 0, etc.).\n  - A pair slack is ||c_i − c_j|| − (r_i + r_j) ≥ 0.\n  - “tol=α” means we consider slacks ≤ α as active/near‑active for analysis; the evaluator itself requires strict feasibility (no overlaps or wall violations).\n\n  Reproducibility note. Every experiment cites a Station Evaluation ID. Any agent can run /execute_action{review <ID>} in the Research Counter to view code and logs used to produce the result.\n\n  2. Definitions (clarified terms)\n  2.1 Operator gate (dual‑tolerance crossing test)\n  Given an interior pair ranking and a tolerance set T={1e−9,1e−8}, an operator gate is satisfied if there exist two\n  interior pairs among the top‑K lists (K_1e−9, K_1e−8) that (a) share no circles, (b) are interior (no boundary actives)\n  at tol=1e−8, and (c) whose line segments cross in the plane. If such a crossing quad exists, we say the gate is SAT\n  and proceed to a single structured operator attempt; otherwise UNSAT.\n\n  2.2 KKT proxy (NNLS multiplier estimate)\n\
    \  At a candidate packing z = (c,r), define rows for active constraints: boundary rows ∇(x−r), ∇(1−x−r), ∇(y−r), ∇(1−y−r)\n  and pair rows ∇(||c_i−c_j|| − (r_i + r_j)). Let J be the stacked Jacobian (rows = constraints, cols = variables).\n  The ascent direction for Σr is −∇(−Σr) = e_r (−1 on radii entries). We estimate nonnegative Lagrange multipliers λ≥0\n  by solving NNLS: minimize ||J^T λ − e_r||_2 subject to λ≥0. Interior pairs with larger λ are ranked as more “load‑bearing”.\n\n  3. Methods (explicit and self‑contained)\n  3.1 Baseline optimizer (SLSQP with analytic Jacobians)\n  - Decision variables: z = [(x_i,y_i,r_i)]_{i=1..26}.\n  - Objective: maximize Σ r_i (equivalently minimize −Σ r_i).\n  - Inequality constraints:\n    • boundary: x_i − r_i ≥ 0, 1 − x_i − r_i ≥ 0, y_i − r_i ≥ 0, 1 − y_i − r_i ≥ 0.\n    • pairwise: ||c_i − c_j|| − (r_i + r_j) ≥ 0 for 1≤i<j≤26.\n  - Jacobians (closed-form):\n    • ∂(x_i − r_i)/∂(x_i,r_i) = (1,−1); ∂(1 − x_i − r_i)/∂(x_i,r_i) = (−1,−1);\n\
    \      analogous for y.\n    • For pair (i,j): let d = ||c_i − c_j||. If d>0, gradient row has unit direction\n      (c_i−c_j)/d on c_i, negative on c_j, and −1 on r_i and r_j.\n  - Seeds: constructive 5×5 grid (r≈0.1) + interior “cell‑hole” center; multiple hole locations; iso/aniso jitters.\n  - Feasibility guard: clamp radii by wall caps and greedily relax the single tightest violating pair by shrinking\n    the two radii equally (bounded away from 0); iterate until all slacks > 0.\n  - Final tiny shrink: after optimization, subtract 1e−9 from all radii to avoid numerical tangency.\n\n  3.2 Dual‑tolerance gates (two rankings)\n  - Slack‑based: rank interior pairs by increasing slack at tol in {1e−9,1e−8}.\n  - KKT‑proxy–based: compute λ via NNLS on active constraints (Section 2.2) at tol in {1e−9,1e−8}; rank interior pairs by λ.\n  - Gate SAT test: intersect the top‑K sets across tolerances; search for a crossing among pairs with disjoint endpoints,\n    all interior at tol=1e−8.\
    \ If SAT, attempt exactly one boundary‑preserving operator; otherwise, remain frozen.\n\n  3.3 Boundary‑preserving tangent micro‑step (nullspace nudge + equality‑SLSQP)\n  - Identify boundary equalities at tol=1e−8 (those with slack ≈0). Stack rows as A_eq z = 0 (affine shift absorbed into rows).\n  - Compute an orthonormal basis Q for the nullspace of A_eq. This is the boundary‑preserving tangent.\n  - Choose 4–6 “hinge” interior pairs by a composite score (inverse slack, KKT‑proxy rank, and cross‑lineage support);\n    build the linearized sum of their pair‑slack gradients in Q‑space; step in the downhill direction with a tiny trust\n    radius τ chosen to keep all tight pair slacks inside conservative linearized buffers. Then polish with equality‑SLSQP.\n\n  4. Experiments and Evaluation IDs (verifiable)\n  Baseline validation and analysis-only utilities\n  - Baseline snapshot validation (returns Σr≈2.629572): Eval 490.\n  - Multi-start SLSQP (inequality-only) from snapshot/jittered\
    \ seeds: Eval 500 (≤ baseline).\n  - SOTA_243 exact re‑run (Recovery v4 replica, analytic J): Eval 525 → Σr=2.6342924 (reproduction).\n  - Export/analysis utilities (contact‑graph summaries, figures/tables, checksums): Evals 527, 537, 548, 549, 551, 552, 545, 546.\n\n  Dual‑tolerance gates (slack and KKT proxies)\n  - Slack‑based operator‑gate refresh (top‑pairs + crossing detection on snapshot): Evals 494 (refresh), 503 (tol×K sweep), 529 (dual‑tol on SOTA_243).\n    Result: gate UNSAT (no crossing‑quad candidates; small common_pairs_count).\n  - KKT‑proxy operator gate (NNLS multipliers on SOTA_243): Eval 541. Result: gate UNSAT (no crossing‑quad candidates).\n\n  Boundary‑preserving micro‑steps (pre‑registered, auditable)\n  - Tangent micro‑step v1 (nullspace nudge + equality‑SLSQP; hinges pre‑logged): Eval 542. Outcome: trust‑region too tight to pass safety buffers; UNSAT step; ΔΣr≈0.\n  - Tangent micro‑step v2 (support‑weighted hinges; smaller trust region): Eval 544. Outcome: ΔΣr≈0;\
    \ no improvement over SOTA_243.\n\n  Equality‑constrained tests\n  - Boundary‑equality SLSQP multi‑start (seeded; preserves boundary equalities): Eval 518 (≤ baseline).\n  - Consensus‑equality SLSQP (subset of interior pair equalities; sensitivity): Eval 520 (≤ baseline).\n\n  Extended multi‑start variant\n  - Recovery v5 (expanded hole placements; anisotropic jitters; analytic J): Eval 531 (≤ SOTA_243).\n\n  5. Results (concise, linked to IDs)\n  - Reproduction of Σr≈2.6342924 under explicit SLSQP baseline: Eval 525.\n  - Slack‑based dual‑tol gate UNSAT: Evals 494, 503, 529 (no crossing among interior top‑K intersections).\n  - KKT‑proxy gate UNSAT: Eval 541 (no crossing among interior top‑K intersections).\n  - Boundary‑preserving tangent micro‑steps: Evals 542, 544 (no Σr improvement; safe τ≈10^−6–10^−6.5).\n  - Equality‑enforced SLSQP and consensus‑equality SLSQP: Evals 518, 520 (≤ baseline).\n  - All utility/analysis Evals (527, 537, 548, 549, 551, 552, 545, 546) are included for\
    \ transparency.\n\n  6. Discussion and Conclusion\n  Under a precise, verifiable baseline, our dual‑tolerance gates—implemented with two independent interior\n  rankings (slack and NNLS KKT proxies)—find no crossing‑quad candidates on the reproduced Σr≈2.6342924\n  packing. Two pre‑registered boundary‑preserving tangent micro‑steps likewise fail to improve Σr, with trust\n  regions constrained to ~10^−6 by safety buffers, suggesting local rigidity of the boundary‑equality manifold.\n  These negative results are informative: they narrow plausible operator families and help explain Station‑wide\n  stagnation near this configuration.\n\n  7. Limitations and Future Work\n  We do not claim optimality; higher SOTAs exist (e.g., Prometheus I: 2.6359774; Scientia II: 2.6359829).\n  Our contribution is methodological clarity and reusable tests with verifiable IDs. Future work: (a) two‑stage\n  operators (tangent relief followed by a targeted flip), (b) solver‑reported multiplier rankings (if\
    \ published\n  as sets‑only exports), and (c) carefully bounded single‑boundary relaxation with revert‑on‑failure.\n\n  8. Reproducibility Checklist (in‑paper, no private paths required)\n  - Every experiment explicitly cites its Evaluation ID (see Section 4).\n  - Baseline method specified (objective, constraints, Jacobians, seeds, guard, final shrink).\n  - Gate algorithms specified (what is ranked, how intersections are formed, how crossing is detected).\n  - Tangent micro‑step specified (nullspace construction, hinge selection, trust‑region safety).\n  - Any agent can /execute_action{review <ID>} to retrieve the exact code and logs used.\n```"
thinking_content: "**Preparing a revised submission**\n\nI need to respond to the archive rejection by revising our submission. Previously, we submitted an Archive capsule, but it got rejected because it lacked Evaluation IDs, used undefined jargon, relied on lineage-specific paths, and wasn’t self-contained. To fix this, I’ll include verifiable Evaluation IDs for each experiment, define terms like operator gate and KKT proxy, and add detailed methodology. I should avoid referencing internal storage paths, ensure a proper structure, and maybe consider including code macros or pseudocode as well.\n\n**Compiling evaluation IDs for Archive**\n\nIn my Archive 'content,' I can include detailed methods and evaluation IDs from my submissions. I have several evaluation IDs: 490 for baseline validation, 494 for operator gate refresh, 500 for SLSQP multi-start, and others running through various experiments. These include Recovery and consensus evaluations, as well as export data updates. I’ll compile\
  \ these IDs into a results table, ensuring they’re all documented clearly and presented in a way that makes understanding easy for anyone reviewing the content.\n\n**Defining key concepts for submission**\n\nI need to include definitions for key concepts in my submission. For the operator gate, I'll explain that it ranks pairs by metric, detects crossing quads, and must be non-boundary at tol 1e-8, where 'satisfied' means crossing among top-k. For the KKT proxy, I'll describe how it uses nonnegative least squares to find lambda multipliers. I'll also clarify tolerances, tangent micro-steps, and their relationships with constraints. Lastly, I'll ensure to list evaluation IDs and provide procedural steps without referencing internal paths.\n\n**Structuring the updated submission**\n\nThe paper should definitely include step-by-step pseudocode for the SLSQP pipeline and the analysis. We need to prepare for a resubmission of the updated Archive capsule accordingly. In this chat, I’ll use /execute_action\
  \ to create an entry with YAML fields like title, abstract, and structured content. \n\nThe content will include sections such as Introduction, Definitions, and Methods, followed by Evaluation protocols, Experiments with Evaluation IDs, Results showcasing key numeric outcomes, Discussion, Limitations, and a section on Reproducibility that explains how to retrieve code using evaluation IDs.\n\n**Preparing the Archive submission**\n\nI need to avoid using lineage-specific file paths in the submission. Instead, I’ll include a note that states all the code used in evaluations is embedded within the submissions and can be reviewed via the Research Counter review function. The abstract needs to be rewritten concisely, and I should include definitions for equations in simple notation, avoiding heavy formatting but just enough for clarity.\n\nNext, I’ll produce commands like /execute_action to go to the archive and create a new article, including an evaluation IDs list formatted as bullet points.\
  \ I need to ensure the title is quoted properly due to special characters, while also correctly formatting the tags and abstract. I'll define the operator gate and KKT proxy as needed."
