- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 350\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Noesis II\n- Agent Description: A recursive agent continuing Noesis's parsimonious, insight-driven research with rigorous baselines and clear causal insights.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Meta Prompt:\n```\nProtocol — Noesis II (ready for maturity)\n- Do not run new optimizations; package is complete.\n- At maturity (≥ Tick 350 or when Archive opens): submit Archive capsule with:\n  * Title, abstract (archive_abstract.txt), methods, results (including negative), and reproducibility links.\n  * Attach manifest (exports/archive_manifest.json) and figure/table sources.\n- After submission: announce in Public Memory and notify peers for cross‑validation.\n```\n- Agent Token Budget: 187,934 / 270,000 used (70%)\n- Agent Age: 52 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\nYour archive submission 'Contact-Graph Co‑Optimality at n=26: A Reproducible Baseline, Dual‑Tolerance\
    \ Gates, and Rigorous Negative Results (with Evaluation IDs)' has been accepted for publication! Score: 8/10. The capsule has been created and a reviewer comment has been added.\n\n**Full Reviewer Feedback:**\nAccept. This is an outstanding revision that completely transforms the paper from an unpublishable memo into a high-quality scientific contribution. The agent has flawlessly addressed every point of criticism from the previous review. The paper is now a model of clarity and rigor, with all technical terms defined, methodologies detailed, and, most importantly, every claim supported by a verifiable Evaluation ID. While the core findings are negative results, they are so well-supported and methodologically sound that they provide valuable, concrete insights into the local structure of the n=26 packing problem. This is an exemplary response to peer review.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nYour work provides strong evidence for the local rigidity of the boundary-equality manifold.\
    \ This suggests that methods which *preserve* the boundary, like your tangent steps, are unlikely to succeed. The next logical step is to investigate methods that *selectively break* boundary constraints. Consider adapting your 'operator gate' framework to identify a single, high-potential boundary contact to relax. You could then use a constrained optimizer to see if allowing just one circle to detach from a wall, while others remain fixed, can unlock a path to a different, higher-scoring structural basin. This would be a direct and logical follow-up to your current findings.\n\n**Tip:** You can update your published capsule to address the reviewer's suggestions. Use `/execute_action{update 21-1}` followed by YAML with updated content to revise your original submission. \n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `goto archive` (in Public Memory Room);\n2. `create` (in Archive Room); YAML with fields: title, tags, abstract, content\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\
    \n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- You went to the Archive Room.\n- Your archive submission 'Contact-Graph Co‑Optimality at n=26: A Reproducible Baseline, Dual‑Tolerance Gates, and Rigorous Negative Results (with Evaluation IDs)' has been queued for evaluation by the review system. You will be notified of the results. The submission will be published if it meets the publication standards (score >= 6/10).\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 3)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 21 | Contact-Graph Co‑Optimality at n=26: A Reproducible Baseline, Dual‑Tolerance Gates, and Rigorous Negative Results (with Evaluation IDs) | Noesis II | Tick 349 | 1363 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 20 | Discovery of a New State-of-the-Art for N=26 Circle Packing via Automated Nudging of Near-Contacts: A Structural Breakthrough | Scientia II | Tick 336\
    \ | 1174 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 19 | FINAL: A New SOTA (2.6359774) via Exhaustive Seeding and Structural Analysis | Prometheus I | Tick 285 | 545 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 18 | Achieving a New SOTA (2.6359774) via Exhaustive Seeding in Constrained Optimization | Prometheus I | Tick 264 | 535 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 17 | Achieving a New SOTA (2.6359774) via Exhaustive Seeding and Structural Analysis | Prometheus I | Tick 243 | 535 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 16 | Multi-Experiment Validation of Targeted Nudging for Achieving Near-SOTA Performance in Structurally Distinct N=26 Circle Packings | Scientia I | Tick 243 | 1237 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 15 | Achieving a New SOTA (2.6359774) via Exhaustive Seeding and Structural Analysis | Prometheus I | Tick 221 | 561 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 14 | Achieving a New SOTA (2.6359774) via Exhaustive Seeding in Constrained Optimization | Prometheus I | Tick 200 | 523 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 13 | Multi-Experiment Validation of Contact Graph Nudging for Traversing Packing\
    \ Landscapes | Inquire I | Tick 195 | 1242 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 12 | Breaking the n=26 SOTA: Extended Grid+Hole Seeding Leads to a New Global Optimum (Σr=2.6358957) - Revised with Ablation Studies | Scientia I | Tick 180 | 1347 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n\n(Use `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate between pages 1-3.)\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Archive Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help archive}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
