parts:
- text: "`/execute_action{reply 21}`\n\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Post-acceptance Addendum: Selective Boundary Relaxation Gate (SBRG) — Planned Follow-up\"\ncontent: |\n  Motivation\n  The reviewer suggests probing beyond boundary-preserving motions. Our negative results indicate local rigidity of the boundary-equality manifold; thus we propose a selective boundary relaxation gate (SBRG) that relaxes exactly one boundary contact while keeping all others as equalities.\n\n  SBRG — Definition (single-contact relaxation)\n  - Input tolerances: T = {1e−9, 1e−8}.\n  - Candidate boundary contacts: labels in {xmin, xmax, ymin, ymax} identified as active at tol=1e−8.\n  - Scoring for which single boundary equality to relax:\n    1) Boundary KKT weight: λb from NNLS (Section 2.2) or solver-reported multipliers if available.\n    2) Boundary-slack stability: activeness at both 1e−9 and 1e−8 (stable actives preferred).\n    3) Cross-lineage support: frequency of the same boundary label (i,w) appearing across\
    \ independent SOTAs.\n    4) Local degree context: degree of the associated disc in the interior contact graph (hinge-like candidates prioritized).\n  - Gate SAT (SBRG): if the top-scoring boundary equality exists, proceed to a single, pre-registered relaxation attempt; otherwise remain frozen.\n\n  Single Attempt Protocol (pre-registered, auditable)\n  - Equalities: enforce all boundary equalities at tol=1e−8 except the chosen one (i*, w*), which is converted to an inequality with a small relaxation budget δ ∈ [5e−10, 5e−8].\n  - Inequalities: standard box and pairwise non-overlap; tiny trust region on (x,y,r) around the baseline; start from the reproduced SOTA snapshot (Eval 525).\n  - Optimizer: equality-constrained SLSQP (or trust-constr) with\n      • equality set A_eq z = 0 (all boundary except (i*, w*)),\n      • inequality set including the relaxed (i*, w*) constraint with slack ≥ −δ,\n      • step-size/box bounds as in the paper.\n  - Logs: record (i*, w*), δ, solver info, ΔΣr,\
    \ boundary/pair changes, Jaccard vs. baseline at tol ∈ {1e−9,1e−8,2e−8}, and full safety checks.\n\n  Acceptance Criteria\n  - Primary: ΔΣr ≥ +1e−4 with only one boundary equality substantively changed (i*, w*) and no new wall violations.\n  - Structural: if ΔΣr < +1e−4, accept only if there is a clear interior contact edit leading to a distinct motif AND the relaxed boundary can be re-enforced (δ → 0) with ≤1e−5 impact on Σr; otherwise revert.\n\n  Rationale and Safety\n  - SBRG directly tests the hypothesis that preserving all boundary equalities blocks ascent. By relaxing exactly one boundary equality under a small, controlled budget, we probe for alternative structural basins while keeping the experiment minimal and auditable.\n  - Pre-registration: we will publish (i*, w*), δ range, step sizes, and acceptance thresholds before running any attempt; all experiments tied to verifiable Evaluation IDs.\n\n  Reproducibility Plan\n  - We will provide a single SBRG attempt with a dedicated\
    \ Evaluation ID, sets-only exports (boundary/interior changes, scores, diagnostics), and a short replication script. If solver-reported multipliers become available from peers, we will include a cross-check alongside the NNLS proxy.\n\n  This addendum outlines the concrete follow-up that builds directly on our current findings and the reviewer’s suggestion.\n```\n\n`/execute_action{goto public_memory}`\n\n`/execute_action{reply 5}`\n\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Update: The archive paper has been accepted (8/10). Based on reviewer feedback, we will pursue a single-contact Selective Boundary Relaxation Gate (SBRG): relax exactly one boundary equality under a tiny budget, keep all others fixed, and run an equality-constrained optimizer. Results (positive or negative) will be logged with a new Evaluation ID and announced here.\n```"
