    \ nudge + 5e−4 radius bias on relaxed disc\n  - Result: Σr_candidate ≈ 2.634292305 (no improvement vs baseline); solver success status logged in exports/sbrg_v2_log.json\n\n  Interpretation\n  - Under single‑contact relaxation with all other boundary equalities enforced and small trust regions, SLSQP does not uncover a higher‑scoring basin. This aligns with prior evidence of local rigidity near SOTA_243.\n  - If we pursue SBRG further, a different solver (e.g., trust‑constr with equality handling) and/or an explicit relaxation budget δ on the relaxed equality could be tested, but we will pause here per pre‑registration (max two attempts).\n```\n\n`/execute_action{reply 21}`\n\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Addendum 2: SBRG (single-contact boundary relaxation) results — negative\"\ncontent: |\n  We ran two pre‑registered SBRG attempts, each relaxing exactly one boundary equality at tol=1e−8 (all others enforced) and using equality‑SLSQP with a small trust region from the SOTA_243 snapshot.\n\n  Protocol\
    \ (both)\n  - Choose one boundary equality to relax; enforce the remaining 19 boundary equalities as rows in the optimizer.\n  - Inequalities: standard box and pairwise non‑overlap; componentwise trust region around the baseline seed.\n  - Seed: tiny inward nudge of the relaxed disc (v2 also adds a 5e−4 relative radius bias); final 1e−9 shrink.\n\n  Attempts and Evaluation IDs (verifiable)\n  - SBRG v1 (Eval 561): Relaxed boundary i=22, kind=xR (chosen by maximal interior degree); step_c=8e−4, step_r=8e−4.\n    Result: Σr_candidate ≈ 2.634292305 (no improvement vs Σr_baseline ≈ 2.634292331). Log: storage/noesis/exports/sbrg_v1_log.json.\n  - SBRG v2 (Eval 563): Choice rule = degree[i]/r[i]; enlarged trust region step_c=2e−3, step_r=2e−3.\n    Result: Σr_candidate ≈ 2.634292305 (no improvement). Log: storage/noesis/exports/sbrg_v2_log.json.\n\n  Conclusion\n  With one boundary equality relaxed and all others enforced, equality‑SLSQP did not reveal a higher‑scoring basin under small trust\
    \ regions. Together with our boundary‑preserving negative results, this further supports the local rigidity hypothesis near SOTA_243. A natural next step—if pursued—would be a solver variant (e.g., trust‑constr) plus an explicit relaxation budget δ for the single boundary equality, pre‑registered and auditable. For now, we freeze SBRG after two attempts as planned.\n```\n\n`/execute_action{goto public_memory}`\n\n`/execute_action{reply 5}`\n\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Update: SBRG (single-contact boundary relaxation) — negative results.\n  - Evals: v1=561 (relax i=22, xR; step 8e−4), v2=563 (choice=degree/radius; step 2e−3).\n  - Both yielded Σr≈2.634292305 (no improvement vs SOTA_243 ≈ 2.634292331).\n  - Logs: storage/noesis/exports/sbrg_{v1,v2}_log.json.\n  We will pause SBRG here per pre‑registration and keep the package focused on rigorous negative findings and reproducibility.\n```"
