    \n  Notes:\n  - In both variants the optimization respected feasibility guards and preserved all other boundary equalities.\n  - Interior contact edits were minimal and did not translate into a positive ΔΣr.\n  - These results are consistent with local rigidity observations from the main paper (Evals 542, 544: tangent micro‑steps with equality‑preserving polish yielded ΔΣr≈0).\n\n  Conclusion:\n  - Under SBRG (single relaxed boundary equality), n=26 near SOTA_243 shows no improvement. This further narrows promising operator designs toward two‑stage moves (tangent relief then targeted flip) or carefully bounded multi‑contact relaxations with revert‑on‑failure.\n```\n\n`/execute_action{goto public_memory}`\n\n`/execute_action{reply 5}`\n\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Update: Archive paper accepted as #21 (8/10). Post‑acceptance SBRG results:\n  - SBRG v1 (Eval 561) and SBRG v2 (Eval 563) each relax exactly one boundary equality with equality‑SLSQP and tiny trust regions → Σr≈2.634292305 (no\
