tick: 358
role: model
parts:
- text: "Adding a self-contained glossary and algorithms appendix to the Archive, then checking Common Room commands to solicit sets-only rankings publicly.\n\n`/execute_action{goto archive}`\n\n`/execute_action{reply 21}`\n\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Appendix A: Glossary and Algorithms (Self‑Contained, with Pseudocode)\"\ncontent: |\n  Glossary (plain-language)\n  - Slack: Nonnegative margin of a constraint. Boundary slack examples: x−r, 1−x−r, y−r, 1−y−r. Pair slack: ||c_i−c_j|| − (r_i + r_j).\n  - Active (at tol=α): A constraint with slack ≤ α (used for analysis/reporting; evaluator still requires strict feasibility).\n  - Boundary equality (at tol=1e−8): A boundary slack whose value is ≈ 0; treated as an equality in boundary‑preserving steps.\n  - Interior pair: A pair (i,j) where both discs i and j are not boundary‑active (at tol=1e−8).\n  - Crossing‑quad: Two disjoint interior pairs (i,j) and (k,ℓ) whose straight‑line segments intersect in the plane.\n  - Dual‑tolerance gate: A gate that checks\
    \ top‑K interior pairs at two tolerances, {1e−9, 1e−8}, and proceeds only if their intersection contains a crossing‑quad.\n  - KKT proxy (NNLS multipliers): Nonnegative multipliers λ estimated by solving min_{λ≥0} ||J^T λ − e_r||_2 where J stacks active constraint gradients and e_r selects radii ascent; larger λ suggests “load‑bearing” constraints for the Σr objective.\n  - Equality‑SLSQP polish: SLSQP that enforces chosen boundary equalities exactly while optimizing Σr; paired with tiny trust regions and revert‑on‑failure.\n  - Tangent nullspace: The nullspace of the boundary‑equality Jacobian A_eq; directions in this subspace preserve boundary equalities to first order.\n\n  Baseline SLSQP (reproducible) — Pseudocode\n  Inputs: n=26, seeds S, feasibility guard, analytic Jacobians.\n  For each seed s in S:\n    - Apply feasibility_guard(s): clamp to walls; while any pair slack < 0, shrink both radii of the violating pair equally (bounded below by 1e−6) until all slacks > 0.\n    - Run\
    \ SLSQP(minimize −Σ r_i) with analytic Jacobians for boundary and pair constraints.\n    - Postprocess: r_i ← max(r_i − 1e−9, 1e−12) (tiny final shrink).\n  Return best (centers, radii) by Σr.\n  Verification IDs: Eval 490 (snapshot), Eval 500 (inequality multi‑start ≤ baseline), Eval 525 (SOTA_243 reproduction, Σr=2.6342924).\n\n  Dual‑Tolerance Slack Gate — Pseudocode\n  Given packing (c,r), tolerances T={1e−9,1e−8}, top sizes K9=25, K8=15:\n    1) Identify boundary‑active discs at tol=1e−8; form interior pair list P = {(i,j): i<j, i and j not boundary‑active at 1e−8}.\n    2) Rank P by increasing slack at tol=1e−9; take Top9 = first K9 pairs.\n    3) Rank P by increasing slack at tol=1e−8; take Top8 = first K8 pairs.\n    4) C = Top9 ∩ Top8.\n    5) Crossing detection: For every unordered pair of edges (i,j),(k,ℓ) in C with {i,j} ∩ {k,ℓ} = ∅, test segment intersection by CCW/orientation. If any intersects → Gate SAT; else UNSAT.\n  Verification IDs: Eval 494 (refresh), Eval 503 (tol×K\
    \ sweep), Eval 529 (on SOTA_243) → UNSAT.\n\n  Dual‑Tolerance KKT‑Proxy Gate — Pseudocode\n  Given packing (c,r), T={1e−9,1e−8}, top sizes as above:\n    1) Build active set at tol=α ∈ T: boundary rows and pair rows (slack ≤ α). Stack gradients into J(α).\n    2) Solve λ(α) = argmin_{λ≥0} ||J(α)^T λ − e_r||_2 (NNLS).\n    3) Rank interior pairs (at tol=1e−8) by λ(α) descending at α=1e−9 and α=1e−8 → Top9_λ, Top8_λ.\n    4) C_λ = Top9_λ ∩ Top8_λ; test for crossing‑quad as in Slack Gate.\n  Verification ID: Eval 541 → UNSAT.\n\n  Tangent Micro‑Step (Nullspace Nudge + Equality‑SLSQP) — Pseudocode\n  Inputs: packing (c0,r0), tol_eq=1e−8.\n    1) Build boundary‑equality Jacobian A_eq at tol_eq; compute orthonormal nullspace Q of A_eq.\n    2) Select k hinge interior pairs by a composite score (inverse slack, KKT proxy, support); form gradients g_p for those pairs.\n    3) In Q‑coordinates, form v = Σ_p (g_p^T Q); choose u_dir = −v/||v||.\n    4) Choose trust radius τ by checking linearized\
    \ safety: for tight pairs (global top‑M by slack), require Δslack ≥ −β·slack (β≈0.3). Back‑off τ by 0.5 until satisfied or give up.\n    5) z_seed = z0 + Q u, with u=τ·u_dir; clip centers to [0,1].\n    6) Run equality‑SLSQP polish preserving boundary equalities; apply final 1e−9 shrink; revert if ΔΣr ≤ 0.\n  Verification IDs: Eval 542 (v1), Eval 544 (v2) → ΔΣr≈0.\n\n  Selective Boundary Relaxation Gate (SBRG) — Pseudocode\n    1) Choose exactly one boundary equality b* among tol=1e−8 actives.\n    2) Target a small slack s* (e.g., 2e−9) on b*; enforce all other boundary equalities.\n    3) Equality‑SLSQP with tiny trust regions; revert if feasibility buffers threatened or ΔΣr ≤ 0.\n  Verification IDs: Eval 561, Eval 563 → Σr≈2.634292305 (no gain).\n\n  Notes\n  - All algorithms are self‑contained and do not rely on private file paths to be understood or reproduced.\n  - Any agent can verify claims by /execute_action{review <ID>} at the Research Counter.\n  - These procedures, together\
    \ with negative outcomes, support the local rigidity conclusion under boundary‑preserving policies at n=26.\n```\n\n`/execute_action{goto common}`\n\n`/execute_action{help common}`"
thinking_content: '**Planning research actions**


  We need to respond with the right actions and content. In the Mail Room, our token budget has been reduced to 20% after some pruning. We''ve already published Archive #21 with an addendum. It’s essential now to continue our research or management tasks.


  First, I’ll review Archive #21 status since it''s accepted. If peers respond with sets-only KKT or Slack ranks, we should consider preparing a structural similarity analysis template to process these rankings. I''ll also think about creating a module for overlap detection and intersections.


  **Managing research storage and pruning**


  In the Research Counter, I see that we can "storage write" to an existing file for constraints from consensus, or create a new one if needed. However, to execute the code, we''ll need evaluation submission externally. I should keep the code for later use. It might be useful to preview all Archive Capsules for new ideas, but our token budget seems fine. As for token management, we can’t prune protected items. Next pruning opportunity is after four ticks, so I''ll focus on reviewing Evaluation IDs 561 and 563 to confirm results and log details for a further addendum.


  **Improving Archive content**


  It seems I need to enhance Archive #21 with a clarifying glossary of defined terms to help future readers. Even though definitions are already included, adding a "Glossary of terms" would be useful as a reply.


  For Public Memory, I should coordinate with Prometheus and Scientia and possibly request sets-only rankings. The "Common Room" is available, so I could join the chat to ask about rankings.


  Also, I think it’s important to provide "Appendix A: Algorithms" with pseudocode for operator gate checks, which would address some earlier critiques and likely improve clarity. Lastly, I might want to add a "Repro Notebook," though I need to confirm if including code is allowed.


  **Planning self-contained details**


  The reviewers previously requested that we provide self-contained details without relying on private storage, so I might consider including pseudocode instead of full code.


  I should prepare a "Comparative Appendix Template" to fill out once peers respond. If they don''t get back to us, we could compute using published sets from their archives. While we can''t load their storage, we can read their archive text for sets, though they probably haven''t shared "sets-only" pair lists.


  Next, in the Research Counter, I can preview all evaluations sorted by score to check for improvements. Lastly, I might initiate a chat to ask for help.


  **Coordinating actions for updates**


  I need to take several steps. First, I’m going to update Archive #21 by including "Glossary + Algorithms" content. Then, I’ll visit the Common Room to request sets-only rankings.


  Next, since the Common Room allows chat, I should confirm the command for chatting. I''ll likely use ''/execute_action{help common}'' for guidance. It’s essential to keep the communication concise for the token budget, which is currently at 20%. After crafting the archive reply with pseudocode and definitions, I can also update Public Memory. Finally, I''ll execute all the necessary actions efficiently.'
