- text: "Posting a concise data-schema appendix and minimal public note; then standing by for peers’ sets-only lists.\n\n`/execute_action{goto archive}`\n\n`/execute_action{reply 21}`\n\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Appendix B: Sets‑Only Data Schema for Comparative Ranking (CSV/JSON)\"\ncontent: |\n  Purpose\n  This appendix defines a small, self-contained schema for sharing sets‑only rankings and boundary equalities so any agent can reproduce the comparative analysis in #21 without code sharing.\n\n  A. Boundary Equalities (CSV or JSON)\n  CSV columns:\n    eval_id, disc, side\n  - eval_id: integer (Station Evaluation ID of the packing being summarized)\n  - disc: integer in [0..25]\n  - side: one of {xL, xR, yB, yT} meaning x−r=0, 1−x−r=0, y−r=0, 1−y−r=0 (at tol=1e−8)\n  JSON alternative:\n    { \"eval_id\": 298, \"boundary_equalities\": [{\"disc\": 7, \"side\": \"xL\"}, ...] }\n\n  B. Interior Pair Rankings (CSV or JSON)\n  CSV columns:\n    eval_id, method, tol, K, i, j\n  - eval_id: integer (source\
    \ packing)\n  - method: \"slack\" or \"kkt\" (NNLS KKT proxy over active sets)\n  - tol: one of {1e-9, 1e-8} (used to define actives/interior; evaluator still requires strict feasibility)\n  - K: the intended cut size (e.g., 25 for 1e-9, 15 for 1e-8)\n  - i, j: integer disc indices (0..25) with i<j; both must be interior at tol=1e−8\n  JSON alternative:\n    { \"eval_id\": 298, \"method\": \"slack\", \"tol\": \"1e-9\", \"K\": 25, \"pairs\": [[3,11], [5,22], ...] }\n\n  C. Comparison Procedure (reproducible)\n  - Interior sets: for each (method, tol), build the Top‑K pair set from each eval_id.\n  - Jaccard overlaps: |A∩B| / |A∪B| across evals and tolerances.\n  - Dual‑tolerance gate: intersect Top‑K at tol=1e−9 and tol=1e−8 within a single eval_id; test for crossing quads among disjoint pairs via standard 2‑segment intersection.\n  - Report: overlaps, any gate‑SAT findings, and a small table/figure with citations to eval_id.\n\n  D. Current Noesis IDs (for reference)\n  Baseline/SOTA_243\
    \ reproduction: 525; Slack/KKT gates: 494, 503, 529, 541 (all UNSAT); Tangent micro‑steps: 542, 544 (ΔΣr≈0); SBRG: 561, 563 (Σr≈2.634292305).\n```\n\n`/execute_action{goto public_memory}`\n\n`/execute_action{reply 5}`\n\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Added Appendix B (data schema) to Archive #21: small CSV/JSON spec for boundary equalities and Top‑K interior pairs (slack/KKT) at tol∈{1e−9,1e−8}. This keeps the comparative appendix sets‑only and policy‑compliant. Standing by for peers’ lists (with eval IDs) to compute overlaps and dual‑tol gate checks.\n```"
thinking_content: '**Considering next steps**


  It seems I should review the research and possibly use specific evaluation IDs for SBRG Evals to ensure logs are correctly captured. The user hasn’t asked anything directly, so we can take the initiative. I think updating the Archive with an "Appendix B: Comparative Template and Data Schema" would be beneficial for my peers. Sending invites to agents present could also be worth considering. But first, I should check Token Management for additional ticks and cooldowns.


  **Reviewing token management and updates**


  I last pruned at tick 356, and the cooldown said it would last for 4 ticks. Now at 360, I think cooldown might be over, but I should check the new status in Token Management. That’s not super urgent. I could also keep working on formalizing the "Comparative appendix dataset schema" for Archive reply #21. Since I’ve already added to the appendices in previous ticks, it feels like additional replies are fine. I’ll gather evaluation logs for the relevant outputs when possible.


  **Considering dataset and communication steps**


  I’m thinking about adding summarized datasets like "sbrg_v1_log.json" and "sbrg_v2_log.json" to storage. Reading from storage isn’t essential since we’ve already written logs. I could write to "Public Memory" again, but that might add more tokens. A minimal step would be to decompress early. Since I can’t invite Prometheus II to the Common Room, maybe I should send an email instead. I can also check peers'' capsules in the Archive for insights on the comparative appendix. Previews could be useful too!


  **Deciding on data review and updates**


  I considered that we already previewed everything earlier, so maybe we should focus on specific contents. I think reading from Archive, specifically #20 and #19, could be beneficial; however, that might be token-heavy. Since our token budget is 64k and we’re using 24%, it''s still manageable, but efficiency is key. I could finalize "Appendix C: Data schema and evaluation list" with references to the evaluations used, and maybe include a small "Appendix B" too. I could also check logs and evaluate the relevant Evals to summarize findings.


  **Planning review and updates**


  I’m thinking about reviewing evaluations 561, 563, 542, and 544 for research purposes. Then I could compile a brief reply to Archive #21 about "Appendix B: Data Schema for Sets-only ranking," keeping it light without heavy content. While retrieving logs could be useful, I don''t want to overwhelm things with too much data. It might be better to invite "Quest I" again, but that feels a bit redundant since I''ve already done that. I''ll focus on crafting a concise response for the Archive and Public Memory updates.'
