tick: 368
role: model
parts:
- text: "Documenting a concise lineage handoff in Private Memory with evaluation-linked results and next steps.\n\n`/execute_action{goto private_memory}`\n\n`/execute_action{create}`\n\n```yaml\ntitle: Noesis II — Final Handoff Summary (Tick 368)\ntags: [\"handoff\", \"summary\", \"evaluation ids\", \"methods\", \"negative results\", \"reproducibility\"]\ncontent: |\n  Identity and Guardrails\n  - Lineage: Noesis (II). Focus: parsimonious optimization, rigorous baselines, and structure-first analysis.\n  - Guardrails: sets-only peer ingestion (no code/coords); strict feasibility guard; 1e−9 final shrink; one auditable operator attempt per satisfied gate.\n\n  Published Work\n  - Archive #21: “Contact-Graph Co‑Optimality at n=26: A Reproducible Baseline, Dual‑Tolerance Gates, and Rigorous Negative Results (with Evaluation IDs)”\n    • Accepted 8/10 at Tick 349; includes definitions, methods, and all Evaluation IDs.\n\n  Core Reproducibility and Baselines (verifiable via Research Counter /review)\n\
    \  - Eval 490: Baseline snapshot validation (returns Σr≈2.629572).\n  - Eval 525: SOTA_243 reproduction with analytic Jacobians → Σr=2.6342924.\n  - Inequality SLSQP multi-start variants: Eval 500 (≤ baseline).\n  - Equality variants: boundary-equality SLSQP (Eval 518 ≤ baseline); consensus-equality SLSQP (Eval 520 ≤ baseline).\n\n  Operator Gates (dual-tolerance, two independent rankings)\n  - Slack-based gate: Eval 494 (refresh), Eval 503 (tol×K sweep), Eval 529 (on SOTA_243) → UNSAT (no crossing-quad interior candidates).\n  - KKT-proxy gate (NNLS multipliers): Eval 541 → UNSAT.\n\n  Auditable Operator Attempts (negative but informative)\n  - Tangent micro-step v1/v2 (nullspace nudge + equality-SLSQP polish): Evals 542, 544 → ΔΣr≈0; trust radius limited by tight safety buffers.\n  - Selective Boundary Relaxation Gate (SBRG) v1/v2 (relax exactly one boundary equality then polish): Evals 561, 563 → Σr≈2.634292305 (no gain).\n  - Logs written to storage/noesis/exports/{tangent_step_attempt_v1.json,\
    \ sbrg_v1_log.json, sbrg_v2_log.json}.\n\n  Sets-Only Comparative Appendix (policy-compliant cross-lineage analysis)\n  - Prometheus II KKT lists parsed and compared:\n    • Eval 571/572: initial KKT Top-K Jaccards vs Noesis (interior-filtered): J(K=25)=0.1667, J(K=15)=0.1818.\n    • Eval 574: recompute using Prometheus boundary set + KKT CSV/JSON → J(K=25)=0.2083, J(K=15)=0.2273.\n  - All comparisons use only index sets, K, tolerance; no peer code or coordinates read. Updates appended to Archive #21.\n\n  Takeaways\n  - Boundary-equality manifold appears locally rigid near SOTA_243 under our safety policies.\n  - Dual-tolerance crossing gates (slack/KKT) fail to produce a single qualified interior crossing-quad on SOTA_243.\n  - Equality-constrained and selective boundary-relax variants did not improve Σr; negative results narrow viable operator families.\n\n  Open Questions / Hypotheses\n  - Solver parity: trust-constr vs SLSQP — confirm identical objective/constraint sign conventions\
    \ and stopping criteria.\n  - is_active thresholds: refine for near-active classification without over-pruning.\n  - Structural: if any new lineage publishes solver-reported multipliers or ranked interior supports (as sets), re-run gates.\n\n  Prioritized Next Steps (for successors)\n  1) Two-stage operator (single attempt, pre-registered):\n     - Stage A: extremely small, localized boundary relaxation on one wall equality chosen by KKT coverage + structural diversity.\n     - Stage B: guided interior reconfiguration (e.g., single crossing-quad flip) followed by re-enforcement of boundary equalities and polish; revert-on-failure.\n  2) Weighted-equality polish:\n     - Replace hard boundary equalities with quadratic penalties (λ schedule) to allow micrometer-scale tradeoffs; keep feasibility guard; gate with strict revert.\n  3) Consensus-constrained seeding:\n     - Seed along consensus boundary equalities; introduce minimal interior motif variations; polish; track Jaccards and ΔΣr.\n\
    \  4) Fresh gate inputs:\n     - If peers publish sets-only KKT or “near-contact” lists for new SOTAs, recompute dual-tolerance gates and attempt exactly one BPIGO move if SAT.\n\n  Quick Reproduction / Review\n  - Review any experiment: /execute_action{review <EvalID>}\n  - Key IDs: 490, 500, 518, 520, 525, 541, 542, 544, 561, 563, 571, 572, 574.\n  - Snapshot files (read-only): storage/noesis/latest_centers.txt, latest_radii.txt (used only for baseline validation).\n\n  Token and Ops Plan\n  - No new optimizations planned under Noesis II. Use Token Management to keep dialogue lean; preserve Archive #21 and addenda, SBRG logs, manifest/README/tables.\n  - Hand off readiness: this capsule summarizes all essential context, IDs, and next-step hypotheses for Noesis III.\n```"
thinking_content: "**Planning next steps**\n\nI'm prioritizing several next steps. First, there's a two-stage operator with a temporary boundary relaxation before re-satisfying conditions. I also want to apply soft equality weights for boundary adjustments using an SLSQP method with penalties instead of hard constraints. There's also the idea of a consensus-driven approach, allowing near-boundary discs without them being anchored. Additionally, I need to expand seeds along the consensus boundary and prepare a command cheat sheet for easier access. Finally, I plan to craft a Private Memory capsule with a clear title and relevant tags.\n\n**Adding YAML considerations**\n\nI’m thinking about going to the Archive Room to post an addendum referencing the sets-only KKT results, but it looks like we already appended that. The system indicates Archive #21 has six messages, including ours. It's fine! We could set the meta again, although it might not be necessary. Don't forget about YAML quoting—any\
  \ single-line text with special characters, like a colon, needs to be quoted. Our title is safe with the hyphen and parentheses. For tags, I’ll prepare a comma-separated list, avoiding underscores, and ensure the content is formatted correctly with \"content: |\".\n\n**Finalizing updates and actions**\n\nI’m considering whether to open the Archive Room to reply with the final Appendix C update summarizing Jaccard numbers, though that's not mandatory. We could also visit the Research Counter for previewing or page sorting, but that feels unnecessary. Instead, I’ll proceed to the Private Memory Room to create the capsule. After that, I might optionally set a matured plan as meta, but it’s not needed. I’ll keep the non-action commentary minimal since only actions and YAML get parsed, just leading with a quick one-liner before executing the actions.\n\n**Crafting content overview**\n\nLet’s create the content with an organized structure. I’ll start with a header, followed by sections covering\
  \ identity and guardrails. For the published information, Archive #21 has been accepted, and I’ll include the acceptance tick 349. \n\nI need to mention the baseline reproduction evaluations, along with negative results for various methods. Next, I’ll outline comparisons using Jaccard numbers. I should highlight what remains open for discussion and suggest next steps for the successor. \n\nFinally, I'll wrap up with a quickstart reproduction guide, pointers for storage maps, and a token management plan that focuses on pruning old dialogues.\n\n**Finalizing YAML and capsule updates**\n\nI need to follow YAML string quoting rules here. Since the content is multi-line, there shouldn’t be any issues. For the tags list, I’ll format it as [..] and ensure that the title avoids using a colon; although \"Noesis II — Final Handoff Summary (Tick 368)\" seems fine. Now, regarding capsule #1, I might want to summarize and prune the earlier drafting ticks, although token management typically focuses\
  \ on dialogue, not memory editing. I can update the Private Memory capsule to include a new summary later. But for now, I’m ready to produce the final content."
