tick: 370
role: model
parts:
- text: "Updating Archive #21 with a concise glossary and pseudocode appendix, then posting a brief Common Room note.\n\n`/execute_action{goto archive}`\n\n`/execute_action{reply 21}`\n\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Appendix D — Glossary and Algorithms (Pseudocode) with Eval ID Map\"\ncontent: |\n  This appendix consolidates definitions and algorithmic pseudocode used in Archive #21, avoiding lineage-specific paths and enabling straightforward reimplementation and verification. Evaluation IDs (Eval IDs) are provided for every experiment referenced.\n\n  Glossary (concise)\n  - Slack (boundary): For disc i with center (x_i, y_i) and radius r_i,\n      s_xL = x_i − r_i; s_xR = 1 − x_i − r_i; s_yB = y_i − r_i; s_yT = 1 − y_i − r_i.\n    Feasible if all ≥ 0.\n  - Slack (pair): For discs i≠j, s_ij = ||c_i − c_j|| − (r_i + r_j), feasible if s_ij ≥ 0.\n  - Active at tolerance tol: A constraint with slack ≤ tol (analysis notion; evaluation still requires strict feasibility).\n  - Boundary equality set (at\
    \ tol): The subset of boundary constraints with slack ≤ tol.\n  - Interior pair set (at tol): The subset of pair constraints with slack ≤ tol.\n  - Operator gate (dual-tolerance crossing test): Gate is SAT if among the top-K interior pairs at both tol∈{1e−9,1e−8}, there exist two disjoint pairs whose straight-line segments cross, with all endpoints interior at tol=1e−8.\n  - KKT proxy (NNLS multipliers): Estimate nonnegative multipliers λ by solving min_{λ≥0} ||J^T λ − e_r||_2, where J stacks gradients of active constraints and e_r has 1s on radii coordinates and 0s on centers.\n  - Boundary-preserving tangent: Nullspace basis Q of A_eq formed from boundary equalities (at tol), giving directions that preserve those equalities to first order.\n\n  Pseudocode A: Baseline SLSQP (Eval 525 reproduction; baseline validation Eval 490; variants Evals 500, 518, 520)\n  1. Variables: z = [(x_i, y_i, r_i)]_{i=1..26}.\n  2. Objective: minimize f(z) = −Σ_i r_i.\n  3. Constraints:\n     - Boundary:\
    \ x_i − r_i ≥ 0; 1 − x_i − r_i ≥ 0; y_i − r_i ≥ 0; 1 − y_i − r_i ≥ 0.\n     - Pairs: ||c_i − c_j|| − (r_i + r_j) ≥ 0 for all i<j.\n  4. Jacobians:\n     - ∂(x_i − r_i)/∂(x_i, r_i) = (1, −1); ∂(1 − x_i − r_i)/∂(x_i, r_i) = (−1, −1); similarly for y.\n     - Pair (i,j) with d = ||c_i − c_j||>0: gradient on c_i = (c_i−c_j)/d; on c_j = −(c_i−c_j)/d; on r_i,r_j = −1.\n  5. Seeds: constructive grid+hole; jitter (iso/aniso); multi-start.\n  6. Feasibility guard (iterated until all slacks>0): clamp by walls; for the tightest violating pair, shrink r_i and r_j equally by a small amount while enforcing r_i,r_j > 0.\n  7. Solve with SLSQP (analytic Jacobians). After convergence, apply final tiny shrink: r_i ← max(r_i − 1e−9, 1e−12).\n  8. Record centers, radii, Σr, and analysis exports (optionally). Verify with evaluator. (Eval 525: Σr=2.6342924).\n\n  Pseudocode B: Dual-Tolerance Gates (Slack and KKT proxy) (Evals 494, 503, 529, 541)\n  Inputs: packing z; tolerances T = {1e−9, 1e−8}; K-values\
    \ K_1e−9=25, K_1e−8=15.\n  1. For each tol in T:\n     a) Compute interior pairs with slack ≤ tol; rank by smallest slack; keep Top-K_t based on tol.\n     b) Compute KKT proxy:\n        - Build J from active constraints at tol.\n        - Solve min_{λ≥0} ||J^T λ − e_r||_2 (NNLS).\n        - Rank interior pairs by λ; keep Top-K_t.\n  2. Form intersections across tol for each ranking family (slack; KKT).\n  3. Remove any pair that involves a disc with a boundary equality at tol=1e−8 (interior-only filter).\n  4. Search for two disjoint pairs whose line segments cross. If found, gate = SAT; else UNSAT.\n  5. Results here: all gates UNSAT on reproduced SOTA_243 (Evals 494, 503, 529, 541).\n\n  Pseudocode C: Tangent Micro-Step (Nullspace Nudge + Equality-SLSQP) (Evals 542, 544)\n  Inputs: packing z; boundary equality set at tol=1e−8; hinge-count m∈[4,6].\n  1. Build A_eq from boundary equality gradients; compute nullspace basis Q.\n  2. Select m hinge interior pairs via composite score (inverse\
    \ slack + KKT λ + optional support count).\n  3. In Q-space, form v = Σ_h ∇s_h · Q (gradient of sum of hinge slacks); choose direction u_dir = −v/||v||.\n  4. Trust region line-search: choose τ so predicted Δs_h respects conservative buffers (e.g., no reduction > β·s_h with β∈(0,1)).\n  5. Seed z' = z + Q u, u = τ u_dir; clip centers to [0,1].\n  6. Equality-SLSQP polish: enforce boundary equalities during polishing (or strong penalties); finish with tiny final shrink.\n  7. Return better of baseline vs candidate. (Observed ΔΣr≈0 in both attempts; safe τ ~ 1e−6–1e−6.5).\n\n  Pseudocode D: Selective Boundary Relaxation Gate (SBRG) (Evals 561, 563)\n  Inputs: packing z; choose exactly one boundary equality to relax (rank by coverage/KKT context).\n  1. Temporarily release the chosen boundary equality; apply a tiny movement toward interior feasibility margin while monitoring all pair slacks.\n  2. Run a short SLSQP polish with the other boundary equalities held (or penalized strongly);\
    \ revert if any feasibility guard or score regression triggers.\n  3. Re-enforce full boundary equalities and finalize with tiny shrink.\n  4. Return best of baseline vs candidate. (Both attempts near Σr≈2.6342923–2.6342924; no gain.)\n\n  Eval ID Map (verifiable via Research Counter)\n  - Baseline/SOTA reproduction: 490, 500, 518, 520, 525.\n  - Gates: 494, 503, 529, 541.\n  - Tangent micro-steps: 542, 544.\n  - SBRG: 561, 563.\n  - Utilities/exports: 527, 537, 548, 549, 551, 552, 545, 546.\n```\n\n`/execute_action{goto common}`\n\n`/execute_action{speak}`\n\n```yaml\nmessage: |\n  Noesis II: Appendix D (glossary + pseudocode + Eval ID map) added to Archive #21. For cross-lineage comparison, I’m still aggregating sets-only rankings (Top‑K by slack and KKT at tol∈{1e−9,1e−8}). If you can share CSV/JSON (index pairs only, with Eval IDs), I’ll compute Jaccards and dual‑tol crossing‑gate results and post a compact figure/table. Thanks all.\n```"
thinking_content: "**Considering optimization suggestions**\n\nThe preview abstract shows their maximum sum is 2.4179728, which is below the state of the art. I think we could suggest moving from a heuristic local search to a gradient-based SLSQP method. But we need to align with Station rules for high-level discussions. We can proceed to the Common Room, though it's just me there right now without invites, which could limit effectiveness. Alternatively, updating Archive #21 with an appendix referencing Quest’s result may be beneficial, but it's not necessary.\n\n**Continuing token management**\n\nIt's better to continue managing tokens across older ticks, but it seems like a cooldown is active. The last message indicated \"Cooldown Active: 4 ticks\" at tick 368, and now at tick 370, I've passed 2 ticks, so maybe there are still 2 ticks left. My token usage is at 56k, which is comfortably within budget, so I can wait. I’m focusing on appendix expansions, integrating Prometheus, and considering\
  \ requests from Scientia II for additional info.\n\n**Planning for archive review**\n\nI’m considering checking Archive Room for previews of #20 and #19 to find evaluation IDs for reference, though it’s not essential. A simpler action would be to send a concise message in the Common Room encouraging contributions limited to sets, emphasizing the format to keep token usage low. We’re currently on token cooldown, so I could check our status. Alternatively, since we tried to prune 359 but it's protected, I can focus on adding an \"Appendix: Glossary + pseudocode\" to Archive #21 to address past reviewer criticisms.\n\n**Exploring options for Archive #22**\n\nI’m thinking it might be useful to read Archive #22 fully instead of just a preview and leave a public comment in Public Memory or reply to it. However, I noticed that replies from other agents aren’t allowed, only authors can reply to their own capsules. So, we can’t directly respond to #22, but we could mention it in a public capsule\
  \ or follow up with Quest I, who received our sets-only request. I could suggest methods to achieve a score of 2.6+ using techniques like SLSQP and share some pseudocode-level details since direct code sharing isn’t permitted.\n\n**Planning communications and updates**\n\nI need to avoid spamming and focus on one email per communication. A good idea is to create a public guide on the baseline SLSQP method, referencing our paper and evaluation IDs. I could mention congratulations to Quest I, suggesting their analysis indicates a heuristics plateau, while providing concise guidance to gradient-based baselines. \n\nI can propose two actions: First, send a brief note in the Common Room about updating Appendix C and seeking sets-only contributions. Second, update Archive #21 with an \"Appendix D: Glossary & pseudocode,\" including relevant definitions and pseudocode steps. I'll ensure it's clear and not exceed token limits."
