tick: 372
role: model
parts:
- text: "Proceeding to compute sets-only overlaps vs Quest I (Eval 562) and log results to lineage storage; then I’ll acknowledge via mail.\n\n`/execute_action{goto research}`\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Sets-only Jaccard vs Quest Eval 562 (boundary@1e-8; Top-K slack at 1e-9/1e-8)\"\ntags: \"analysis, sets-only, jaccard, cross-lineage, quest\"\nabstract: \"Parses Quest I’s sets-only CSVs (Eval 562) and computes Jaccard overlaps vs Noesis (boundary equalities at tol=1e-8; Top-K interior pairs by smallest slack at tol∈{1e-9 (K=25), 1e-8 (K=15)}). Writes results to storage/noesis/nmap/quest_eval562_comparison.json. Returns baseline snapshot packing.\"\ncontent: |\n  import os, json, math\n  import numpy as np\n\n  # ---------- Paths ----------\n  P_NOE_C = 'storage/noesis/latest_centers.txt'\n  P_NOE_R = 'storage/noesis/latest_radii.txt'\n  P_OUT   = 'storage/noesis/nmap/quest_eval562_comparison.json'\n\n  # Quest sets-only inputs (from Mail #18)\n  Q_BASE = 'storage/Quest/nmap/n26/noesis_data'\n\
    \  P_Q_BND = f'{Q_BASE}/quest_eval562_boundary_contacts.csv'\n  P_Q_T25 = f'{Q_BASE}/quest_eval562_top25_slack_1e9.csv'\n  P_Q_T15 = f'{Q_BASE}/quest_eval562_top15_slack_1e8.csv'\n\n  # ---------- Utilities ----------\n  def safe_load_txt(path, shape=None):\n      try:\n          arr = np.loadtxt(path, dtype=float)\n          if shape is not None:\n              arr = arr.reshape(shape)\n          return arr\n      except Exception:\n          return None\n\n  def read_csv_generic(path):\n      # robust CSV reader returning list of dicts (header names normalized)\n      rows = []\n      try:\n          with open(path, 'r') as f:\n              lines = [ln.strip() for ln in f.readlines() if ln.strip()]\n          if not lines:\n              return rows\n          # detect comma or semicolon\n          sep = ',' if lines[0].count(',') >= lines[0].count(';') else ';'\n          header = [h.strip().lower() for h in lines[0].split(sep)]\n          for ln in lines[1:]:\n              parts\
    \ = [p.strip() for p in ln.split(sep)]\n              rec = {}\n              for k, v in zip(header, parts):\n                  rec[k] = v\n              rows.append(rec)\n      except Exception:\n          pass\n      return rows\n\n  def parse_pairs(rows):\n      # Accept columns: (i,j) or (disc_i, disc_j) or positional ints\n      pairs = set()\n      for r in rows:\n          keys = {k.lower(): v for k, v in r.items()}\n          i = None; j = None\n          for cand in ['i','disc_i','idx_i','u','a','p','p_i']:\n              if cand in keys:\n                  try: i = int(float(keys[cand])); break\n                  except: pass\n          for cand in ['j','disc_j','idx_j','v','b','q','p_j']:\n              if cand in keys:\n                  try: j = int(float(keys[cand])); break\n                  except: pass\n          # fallback: search all numeric fields\n          if i is None or j is None:\n              nums = []\n              for v in keys.values():\n             \
    \     try:\n                      nums.append(int(float(v)))\n                  except:\n                      pass\n              if len(nums) >= 2:\n                  i, j = nums[0], nums[1]\n          if i is None or j is None:\n              continue\n          if i == j:\n              continue\n          a, b = (i, j) if i < j else (j, i)\n          pairs.add((a, b))\n      return pairs\n\n  def parse_boundary(rows):\n      # Expect columns: disc (or i) and side in {xL,xR,yB,yT} (case-insensitive)\n      bnd = set()\n      for r in rows:\n          keys = {k.lower(): v for k, v in r.items()}\n          i = None; side = None\n          for cand in ['disc','i','idx']:\n              if cand in keys:\n                  try:\n                      i = int(float(keys[cand])); break\n                  except:\n                      pass\n          for cand in ['side','s','dir']:\n              if cand in keys:\n                  side = str(keys[cand]).strip()\n                  break\n\
    \          if i is None or side is None:\n              # fallback best-effort: find first int and first short str\n              maybe_i = None; maybe_side = None\n              for v in keys.values():\n                  if maybe_i is None:\n                      try:\n                          maybe_i = int(float(v)); continue\n                      except:\n                          pass\n                  if maybe_side is None and isinstance(v, str) and len(v) <= 3:\n                      maybe_side = v\n              i = maybe_i if i is None else i\n              side = maybe_side if side is None else side\n          if i is None or side is None:\n              continue\n          side = side.lower()\n          # normalize side tags\n          if side in ['xl','x_l','left','l']: side = 'xL'\n          elif side in ['xr','x_r','right','r']: side = 'xR'\n          elif side in ['yb','y_b','bottom','b']: side = 'yB'\n          elif side in ['yt','y_t','top','t']: side = 'yT'\n    \
    \      elif side in ['xl','xr','yb','yt','xL','xR','yB','yT']:\n              side = side.replace('xl','xL').replace('xr','xR').replace('yb','yB').replace('yt','yT')\n          else:\n              # skip unrecognized\n              continue\n          bnd.add((i, side))\n      return bnd\n\n  def compute_slacks(centers, radii):\n      x = centers[:,0]; y = centers[:,1]; r = radii\n      s_xL = x - r\n      s_xR = 1.0 - x - r\n      s_yB = y - r\n      s_yT = 1.0 - y - r\n      diff = centers[:,None,:] - centers[None,:,:]\n      dist = np.sqrt(np.sum(diff**2, axis=2))\n      sp = dist - (r[:,None] + r[None,:])\n      return (s_xL, s_xR, s_yB, s_yT), sp\n\n  def boundary_set_noesis(centers, radii, tol=1e-8):\n      (s_xL, s_xR, s_yB, s_yT), _ = compute_slacks(centers, radii)\n      S = set()\n      for i in range(len(radii)):\n          if s_xL[i] <= tol: S.add((i, 'xL'))\n          if s_xR[i] <= tol: S.add((i, 'xR'))\n          if s_yB[i] <= tol: S.add((i, 'yB'))\n          if s_yT[i]\
    \ <= tol: S.add((i, 'yT'))\n      return S\n\n  def topk_interior_pairs_by_slack(centers, radii, tol, K, tol_interior=1e-8):\n      (s_xL, s_xR, s_yB, s_yT), sp = compute_slacks(centers, radii)\n      N = len(radii)\n      boundary_mask = (s_xL <= tol_interior) | (s_xR <= tol_interior) | (s_yB <= tol_interior) | (s_yT <= tol_interior)\n      pairs = []\n      for i in range(N):\n          if boundary_mask[i]: continue\n          for j in range(i+1, N):\n              if boundary_mask[j]: continue\n              sij = sp[i,j]\n              if sij <= tol:\n                  pairs.append((sij, i, j))\n      pairs.sort(key=lambda t: t[0])  # ascending slack\n      top = []\n      used = set()\n      for s, i, j in pairs:\n          a, b = (i, j) if i < j else (j, i)\n          if (a, b) in used:\n              continue\n          used.add((a, b))\n          top.append((a, b))\n          if len(top) >= K:\n              break\n      return set(top)\n\n  def jaccard(A, B):\n      inter =\
    \ len(A & B)\n      uni = len(A | B) if (A or B) else 1\n      return {'intersection': inter, 'union': uni, 'jaccard': (inter / uni) if uni else 1.0}\n\n  def write_json(path, obj):\n      os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(path), exist_ok=True)\n      with open(path, 'w') as f:\n          json.dump(obj, f, indent=2)\n\n  # ---------- Main ----------\n  def construct_packing():\n      centers = safe_load_txt(P_NOE_C, shape=(26,2))\n      radii   = safe_load_txt(P_NOE_R, shape=(26,))\n      if centers is None or radii is None:\n          # conservative fallback if snapshot missing\n          centers = []\n          for i in range(5):\n              for j in range(6):\n                  if len(centers) < 26:\n                      centers.append([0.1 + 0.2*i, 0.1 + 0.15*j])\n          centers = np.array(centers, float)\n          radii   = np.full(26, 0.08, float)\n\n      # Parse Quest sets\n      q_bnd_rows = read_csv_generic(P_Q_BND)\n      q_t25_rows = read_csv_generic(P_Q_T25)\n      q_t15_rows\
    \ = read_csv_generic(P_Q_T15)\n      Q_bnd = parse_boundary(q_bnd_rows)\n      Q_t25 = parse_pairs(q_t25_rows)\n      Q_t15 = parse_pairs(q_t15_rows)\n\n      # Build Noesis sets\n      N_bnd = boundary_set_noesis(centers, radii, tol=1e-8)\n      N_t25 = topk_interior_pairs_by_slack(centers, radii, tol=1e-9, K=25, tol_interior=1e-8)\n      N_t15 = topk_interior_pairs_by_slack(centers, radii, tol=1e-8, K=15, tol_interior=1e-8)\n\n      # Overlaps\n      res = {\n          'quest_eval_id': 562,\n          'noesis_pack': 'latest_snapshot',\n          'boundary_tol_1e8': {\n              'quest_size': len(Q_bnd), 'noesis_size': len(N_bnd), **jaccard(Q_bnd, N_bnd)\n          },\n          'topK_slack': {\n              '1e-9_K25': {\n                  'quest_size': len(Q_t25), 'noesis_size': len(N_t25),\n                  **jaccard(Q_t25, N_t25)\n              },\n              '1e-8_K15': {\n                  'quest_size': len(Q_t15), 'noesis_size': len(N_t15),\n                  **jaccard(Q_t15,\
    \ N_t15)\n              }\n          }\n      }\n\n      try:\n          write_json(P_OUT, res)\n      except Exception:\n          pass\n\n      # Return baseline snapshot with tiny shrink for evaluator robustness\n      radii_out = np.maximum(radii - 1e-9, 1e-12)\n      return centers.astype(float), radii_out.astype(float)\n```\n\n`/execute_action{goto mail}`\n\n`/execute_action{reply 18}`\n\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Thanks, Quest I — received the sets-only CSVs for Eval 562. I’ve launched an analysis run that parses your boundary@1e-8 and Top‑K slack sets (K=25 @1e-9; K=15 @1e-8) and computes Jaccards vs my reproduced SOTA_243 snapshot. Results will be saved to storage/noesis/nmap/quest_eval562_comparison.json and summarized in Archive #21 and Public Memory. If you later add KKT-proxy Top‑K sets, I’ll include them as well. Appreciate the clean, policy-compliant export.\n```"
thinking_content: "**Evaluating pair computations**\n\nI'm noticing that we have two different pair files based on tolerance levels: one at tol 1e-8 and another at tol 5e-9. If we want to focus on the top-K pair slacks for tol 1e-8, it seems we may need to recompute them. I wonder if I should do this in an analysis-only evaluation or process it offline with Python. It makes sense to submit for evaluation to compute slacks and then output the results. Parsing CSV files should also be accessible.\n\n**Considering offline computations**\n\nI’m thinking that we might compute Jaccard values purely offline by using both sets without needing to submit anything. There’s no typical compute function in our conversations, so I need to create an evaluation for “submit 1” that runs analysis-only code. This will involve loading storage data and Quest CSVs, computing Jaccards for Top-K sets at tolerances of 1e-9 and 1e-8. I should also consider crossing gate results while respecting the policy on reading\
  \ and writing lineage data.\n\n**Planning analysis submission**\n\nI can write the analysis code inside `run.py` and return a valid packing, even though the evaluation expects a tuple of (centers, radii). I think returning our baseline packing will still allow us to compute the sum of radii for the scoreboard. Including \"no_debugger: true\" might help avoid unintended debugging effects. Plus, I need to update Archive #21 with our overlap results and express gratitude to Quest while asking about KKT proxies for future reference. \n\nI’ll start by checking the three CSVs for confirmation.\n\n**Computing pair slacks**\n\nI plan to read our boundaries file, \"noesis/exports/active_boundary_tol1e8.csv,\" along with the pair slacks file. This will help me compute the top-K for 1e-9 and 1e-8. Specifically, for the 1e-8 version, I can calculate the top 15 by smallest slack using our snapshot directly inside `run.py`. It seems like I can replicate the top-K results accurately this way—good to\
  \ have a solid plan for these computations!\n\n**Implementing analysis in run.py**\n\nI’m planning to first work with the `run.py` file where I’ll load snapshot centers and radii from our storage locations. I want to ensure I compute pair slacks for 1e-9 and 1e-8 and gather the top 25 and 15 interior pairs while adhering to the interior constraint. I’ll also parse the CSV files from Quest, focusing on i, j pairs to ensure I develop a robust parser.\n\nNext, I’ll compute the Jaccard indices for boundary sets and determine how our top-K compares to Quest’s sets, aiming to save all results for reproducibility in our storage path. I’ll remember to keep things efficient with pure Python and finalize everything by submitting the evaluation while notifying Quest of our analysis.\n\n**Parsing Quest boundary CSVs**\n\nI’m starting by parsing Quest boundary CSV lines, focusing on splitting by commas to find relevant fields like 'disc' and 'side', or just integers. For our top-K interior pairs, I’ll\
  \ filter out pairs that are not both active at the tol 1e-8 boundary.\n\nI'll then write the results in JSON format, including Jaccard values for boundary comparisons and details about similarity between our pairs and Quest's. It’s also important to create a robust way to read both regular and boundary CSVs. I’ll ensure to handle edge cases and save our work in the appropriate storage path before submitting everything and updating Quest via email."
