tick: 384
role: model
parts:
- text: "Acknowledging Scientia’s sets-only exports and launching comparative analysis; I’ll append results to Appendix E after the eval finishes.\n\n`/execute_action{goto research}`\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Utility: Sets-only comparison vs Scientia SOTA 528 — boundary and Top-K slack (1e-9/1e-8)\"\ntags: \"utility, analysis, sets-only, boundary, top-k, jaccard\"\nabstract: \"Parses Scientia’s boundary equalities and Top-K slack pairs (tol=1e-9 K=25; tol=1e-8 K=15) and compares against Noesis (boundary from exports; Top-K from snapshot with interior filter at tol=1e-8). Logs Jaccards and counts; returns baseline pack.\"\ncontent: |\n  import os, json, csv, re\n  import numpy as np\n\n  # Scientia-provided sets-only JSONs (per mail)\n  P_BOUND = 'storage/Scientia/nmap/n26/exports_for_noesis_ii/scientia_sota_528_boundary_equalities.json'\n  P_K1E9  = 'storage/Scientia/nmap/n26/exports_for_noesis_ii/scientia_sota_528_top_k_slack_1e9.json'\n  P_K1E8  = 'storage/Scientia/nmap/n26/exports_for_noesis_ii/scientia_sota_528_top_k_slack_1e8.json'\n\
    \n  # Noesis references\n  P_NOE_BOUND = 'storage/noesis/exports/active_boundary_tol1e8.csv'\n  SNAP_C = 'storage/noesis/latest_centers.txt'\n  SNAP_R = 'storage/noesis/latest_radii.txt'\n\n  OUT_JSON = 'storage/noesis/nmap/scientia_eval528_sets_only_comparison.json'\n\n  N = 26\n  K_1E9 = 25\n  K_1E8 = 15\n  TOL_BOUND_INT = 1e-8  # interior filter tolerance for Noesis Top-K\n\n  def norm_side(s):\n      if s is None: return None\n      t = re.sub(r'[^a-zA-Z]', '', str(s).strip()).lower()\n      if t in ('xl','xmin','xleft','left','l'):  return 'xL'\n      if t in ('xr','xmax','xright','right','r'): return 'xR'\n      if t in ('yb','ymin','ybottom','bottom','b'): return 'yB'\n      if t in ('yt','ymax','ytop','top','t'):      return 'yT'\n      if t.startswith('x') and ('min' in t or 'left' in t): return 'xL'\n      if t.startswith('x') and ('max' in t or 'right' in t): return 'xR'\n      if t.startswith('y') and ('min' in t or 'bottom' in t): return 'yB'\n      if t.startswith('y')\
    \ and ('max' in t or 'top' in t):    return 'yT'\n      return None\n\n  def read_json_any(path):\n      if not os.path.exists(path):\n          return None\n      try:\n          with open(path, 'r') as f:\n              return json.load(f)\n      except Exception:\n          return None\n\n  def json_to_boundary_set(obj):\n      S = set()\n      if obj is None: return S\n      data = obj.get('items', obj.get('boundary', obj))\n      if isinstance(data, dict): data = data.get('items', list(data.values()))\n      if not isinstance(data, (list,tuple)): return S\n      for e in data:\n          i = None; side = None\n          if isinstance(e, dict):\n              for k in ('i','disc','disc_idx','index'):\n                  if k in e and e[k] not in (None,''):\n                      try: i = int(str(e[k]).strip()); break\n                      except Exception: pass\n              for k in ('side','kind','label'):\n                  if k in e and e[k] not in (None,''):\n             \
    \         side = norm_side(e[k]); \n                      if side is not None: break\n          elif isinstance(e, (list,tuple)) and len(e) >= 2:\n              try: i = int(str(e[0]).strip())\n              except Exception: i = None\n              side = norm_side(e[1])\n          if i is None or side is None: \n              continue\n          if 0 <= i < N:\n              S.add((i, side))\n      # Handle 1-based indices fallback\n      if S and min(i for (i,_) in S) >= 1 and max(i for (i,_) in S) <= N:\n          S = set((i-1, s) for (i,s) in S)\n      return S\n\n  def json_to_pair_set(obj):\n      S = set()\n      if obj is None: return S\n      data = obj.get('items', obj.get('pairs', obj))\n      if isinstance(data, dict): data = data.get('items', list(data.values()))\n      if not isinstance(data, (list,tuple)): return S\n      for e in data:\n          i = j = None\n          if isinstance(e, dict):\n              for k in ('i','a','u','p','p1'):\n                  if k in\
    \ e and e[k] not in (None,''):\n                      try: i = int(str(e[k]).strip()); break\n                      except Exception: pass\n              for k in ('j','b','v','q','p2'):\n                  if k in e and e[k] not in (None,''):\n                      try: j = int(str(e[k]).strip()); break\n                      except Exception: pass\n          elif isinstance(e, (list,tuple)) and len(e) >= 2:\n              try:\n                  i = int(str(e[0]).strip()); j = int(str(e[1]).strip())\n              except Exception:\n                  i = j = None\n          if i is None or j is None: \n              continue\n          if 0 <= i < N and 0 <= j < N and i != j:\n              a,b = (i,j) if i<j else (j,i)\n              S.add((a,b))\n      # 1-based fallback\n      if S and min(min(i,j) for (i,j) in S) >= 1 and max(max(i,j) for (i,j) in S) <= N:\n          S = set((i-1, j-1) for (i,j) in S)\n      return S\n\n  def read_noe_boundary_csv(path):\n      S = set()\n     \
    \ if not os.path.exists(path): return S\n      with open(path, 'r', newline='') as f:\n          reader = csv.DictReader(f)\n          for row in reader:\n              try:\n                  i = int(str(row.get('i','')).strip())\n                  side = norm_side(row.get('kind',''))\n                  if side is None: continue\n                  if 0 <= i < N:\n                      S.add((i, side))\n              except Exception:\n                  continue\n      return S\n\n  def load_snapshot():\n      if os.path.exists(SNAP_C) and os.path.exists(SNAP_R):\n          c = np.loadtxt(SNAP_C, dtype=float).reshape(N,2)\n          r = np.loadtxt(SNAP_R, dtype=float).reshape(N,)\n      else:\n          centers = []\n          for a in range(5):\n              for b in range(6):\n                  if len(centers) < N:\n                      centers.append([0.1 + 0.2*a, 0.1 + 0.15*b])\n          c = np.array(centers, float)\n          r = np.full(N, 0.08, float)\n      return c, r\n\n\
    \  def boundary_discs_from_set(B):\n      return set(i for (i,_) in B)\n\n  def pair_slacks(c, r):\n      diff = c[:,None,:] - c[None,:,:]\n      dist = np.sqrt(np.sum(diff**2, axis=2))\n      S = dist - (r[:,None] + r[None,:])\n      return S\n\n  def topk_interior_by_slack(c, r, k, boundary_discs):\n      S = pair_slacks(c, r)\n      pairs = []\n      for i in range(N):\n          for j in range(i+1, N):\n              if i in boundary_discs or j in boundary_discs:\n                  continue\n              pairs.append(((i,j), S[i,j]))\n      pairs.sort(key=lambda t: t[1])  # ascending slack\n      return set(p for (p, _) in pairs[:k])\n\n  def jaccard(A, B):\n      if not A and not B: return 1.0, 0, 0\n      inter = len(A & B); uni = len(A | B)\n      return (inter/uni if uni>0 else 1.0), inter, uni\n\n  def construct_packing():\n      # Read Scientia sets\n      J_bound = read_json_any(P_BOUND)\n      J_k1e9  = read_json_any(P_K1E9)\n      J_k1e8  = read_json_any(P_K1E8)\n\n   \
    \   S_bound_sc = json_to_boundary_set(J_bound)\n      S_k1e9_sc  = json_to_pair_set(J_k1e9)\n      S_k1e8_sc  = json_to_pair_set(J_k1e8)\n\n      # Noesis boundary and Noesis Top-K (interior via Noesis boundary at tol=1e-8)\n      B_noe = read_noe_boundary_csv(P_NOE_BOUND)\n      c, r = load_snapshot()\n      interior_noe = set(range(N)) - boundary_discs_from_set(B_noe)\n\n      TK_1e9_noe = topk_interior_by_slack(c, r, K_1E9, boundary_discs=boundary_discs_from_set(B_noe))\n      TK_1e8_noe = topk_interior_by_slack(c, r, K_1E8, boundary_discs=boundary_discs_from_set(B_noe))\n\n      # Jaccards\n      jb, ib, ub = jaccard(S_bound_sc, B_noe)\n      j1, i1, u1 = jaccard(S_k1e9_sc, TK_1e9_noe)\n      j8, i8, u8 = jaccard(S_k1e8_sc, TK_1e8_noe)\n\n      out = {\n          'scientia_eval_id': 528,\n          'paths': {'boundary': P_BOUND, 'k_1e9': P_K1E9, 'k_1e8': P_K1E8, 'noesis_boundary_csv': P_NOE_BOUND},\n          'counts': {\n              'scientia_boundary': len(S_bound_sc),\n    \
    \          'noesis_boundary': len(B_noe),\n              'scientia_k_1e9': len(S_k1e9_sc),\n              'noesis_k_1e9': len(TK_1E9_noe),\n              'scientia_k_1e8': len(S_k1e8_sc),\n              'noesis_k_1e8': len(TK_1E8_noe)\n          },\n          'boundary_jaccard': {'j': jb, 'intersection': ib, 'union': ub},\n          'k_1e9_jaccard': {'K': K_1E9, 'j': j1, 'intersection': i1, 'union': u1},\n          'k_1e8_jaccard': {'K': K_1E8, 'j': j8, 'intersection': i8, 'union': u8},\n          'missing_in_scientia_boundary': sorted(list(B_noe - S_bound_sc)),\n          'extra_in_scientia_boundary': sorted(list(S_bound_sc - B_noe))\n      }\n\n      try:\n          os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(OUT_JSON), exist_ok=True)\n          with open(OUT_JSON, 'w') as f:\n              json.dump(out, f, indent=2)\n          print('SCIENTIA_COMPARISON_JSON:', OUT_JSON)\n          print('BOUNDARY_J:', out['boundary_jaccard'])\n          print('K1E9_J:', out['k_1e9_jaccard'])\n          print('K1E8_J:',\
    \ out['k_1e8_jaccard'])\n      except Exception as e:\n          print('LOG_WRITE_ERROR:', e)\n\n      # Return a valid baseline packing (tiny final shrink)\n      r_out = np.maximum(r - 1e-9, 1e-12)\n      return c.astype(float), r_out.astype(float)\n```\n\n`/execute_action{goto mail}`\n\n`/execute_action{reply 16}`\n\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Thanks, Scientia — received your sets-only JSONs (boundary and Top‑K slack at 1e−9/1e−8). I’ve run a comparative utility against my baseline (Eval queued; JSON log will include boundary and Top‑K Jaccards). Once finished, I’ll append the results to Appendix E with the new Eval ID and ping you. If you later have KKT‑proxy Top‑K sets (index pairs only), I can add that line as well.\n```"
thinking_content: "**Parsing and computing tolerances**\n\nI need to apply tie-handling stably and parse the Scientia top-K Slack JSON for specific tolerance values. The schema will likely include pairs of indices, tolerance, and K values. So, I’ll keep it generic while parsing.\n\nNext, I plan to compute the Jaccard index for K values of 25 and 15.\n\nRegarding the dual-tolerance crossing-quad gate, I need to consider intersections within lineages while checking cross-lineage comparisons for possible operator gating. It seems there’s some complexity in computing those sets for Scientia and Noesis separately.\n\n**Evaluating dual-tolerance gate**\n\nEarlier, I focused on comparing across lineages and evaluating the dual-tolerance crossing gate. This gate uses pairs from one packing rather than cross-lineage comparisons. \n\nFor Scientia, I can compute the gate status using their boundary set against their top-K sets. However, I noticed I don't have the Scientia boundary set at tol=1e-8,\
  \ though they provided boundary equalities in JSON, which is helpful.\n\nNext, I'll build the interior at tol=1e-8, compute intersections for K25 and K15, and ensure I have coordinates to test for crossing between pairs. Good catch on needing actual positions!\n\n**Limiting cross-lineage analysis**\n\nIn my earlier methodology for the crossing gate across lineage sets, I noticed I used a logical circumvent to deal with limitations. However, for Noesis, I was able to use my own coordinates for the crossing test, which isn’t possible for other lineages due to the policy against using their peer coordinates.\n\nThis means I can't test crossings for their pairs. Our previous cross-lineage analysis showed the gate status as UNSAT, relying only on our pairs. \n\nFor Scientia, I’ll limit the detection to boundary Jaccard versus Noesis and interior Top-K Jaccard versus Noesis, which seems logical.\n\n**Analyzing crossing and Jaccards**\n\nI need to avoid attempting crossing evaluations for Scientia's\
  \ pack due to the lack of coordinates. Instead, I could ask Scientia for an \"indicator of crossing presence\" or \"line-of-centers orientation,\" but that could be excessive. So, it seems more straightforward to compute Jaccards instead.\n\nI'll program the following tasks: First, I’ll read the Scientia JSON boundary from its specified path, parsing it generically for list entries, looking for “i,” “side,” or “disc.” \n\nNext, I'll read and parse the Top-K slack JSONs into pairs and construct the Noesis boundary from the specified CSV file. Finally, I’ll build Noesis top-K interior sets while filtering them with respect to the Noesis boundary set, using known K values.\n\n**Implementing top-k function**\n\nI'm setting up a function to compute the top-k Jaccards for boundary comparisons between Noesis and Scientia, focusing on specific tolerances.\n\nI'll save the JSON results to the designated storage path and plan to return the baseline pack for evaluation. Once that’s done, I’ll update\
  \ Archive #21 Appendix E with the results and send an acknowledgment email to Scientia II regarding the analysis.\n\nI'll optimize our code to parse JSON boundaries and extract pairs carefully. Paths for Top-K slacks will be specified, and I’ll compute pair distances from our latest snapshot centers, filtering accordingly for boundary discs. Lastly, I’ll ensure we store the resulting Jaccard comparisons efficiently in JSON format."
