parts:
- text: "Identity: Noesis II. Location: Archive Room. Recap: Appendix E v1 (sets-only comparative analysis) is finalized; README committed and verified. I received an inactivity notice—so I’ll run a lightweight, useful analysis experiment: a Gate v1 sensitivity sweep (tolerance and crossing criterion) on our snapshot. This keeps research momentum without new optimization, aligns with our maintenance plan, and may surface thresholds under which a SAT emerges on our geometry.\n\nReflection: Our sets-only program established strong boundary consensus at tol=2e−8 and modest interior overlaps, and revealed geometry/criterion sensitivity in gate outcomes. A systematic sensitivity curve for Gate v1 on our snapshot will turn anecdotes into a reproducible diagnostic, helping successors quickly assess when/if a single operator attempt is justified. I’ll log a compact JSON with counts and SAT/UNSAT across tolerance and crossing variants, then append results to Appendix E.\n\n`/execute_action{goto research}`\n\
    \n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Gate v1 Sensitivity Sweep on Noesis Snapshot: tol_grid × crossing(strict|inclusive)\"\ntags: \"utility, sets-only, gate, tolerance-sweep, crossing, analysis\"\nabstract: \"Compute Gate v1 outcomes on the Noesis snapshot across tol_rank ∈ {1e-9,1e-8} (Top-K K25/K15), interior_filter ∈ {1e-9,2e-9,5e-9,1e-8,2e-8}, and crossing criteria (strict vs inclusive). Logs sizes, intersections, interior counts, and SAT/UNSAT to JSON; returns baseline pack.\"\ncontent: |\n  import os, json, math\n  import numpy as np\n\n  # Parameters (Gate v1 canonical)\n  TOL_RANKS = [1e-9, 1e-8]        # for building Top-K lists (K25 @ 1e-9, K15 @ 1e-8)\n  KS        = {1e-9: 25, 1e-8: 15}\n  TOL_FILTERS = [1e-9, 2e-9, 5e-9, 1e-8, 2e-8]  # for interior filter on boundary discs\n\n  N = 26\n  SNAP_C = 'storage/noesis/latest_centers.txt'\n  SNAP_R = 'storage/noesis/latest_radii.txt'\n  OUT_JSON = 'storage/noesis/nmap/gate_sensitivity_v1_noesis.json'\n\n  # -----------------\
    \ Geometry helpers -----------------\n  def slacks(c, r):\n      x = c[:,0]; y = c[:,1]\n      s = {\n          'xL': x - r,\n          'xR': 1.0 - x - r,\n          'yB': y - r,\n          'yT': 1.0 - y - r\n      }\n      diff = c[:,None,:] - c[None,:,:]\n      dist = np.sqrt(np.sum(diff**2, axis=2))\n      sp = dist - (r[:,None] + r[None,:])\n      return s, sp\n\n  def boundary_active(s, tol):\n      active = np.zeros((N,), dtype=bool)\n      # A disc is boundary-active if any wall is <= tol\n      for k in ('xL','xR','yB','yT'):\n          active |= (s[k] <= tol + 1e-12)\n      return active\n\n  def topk_pairs_by_slack(sp, tol_rank, K):\n      pairs = []\n      for i in range(N):\n          for j in range(i+1, N):\n              sij = float(sp[i,j])\n              if sij <= tol_rank + 1e-12:\n                  pairs.append((sij, i, j))\n      pairs.sort(key=lambda t: t[0])\n      if K is None:\n          return [(i,j) for _,i,j in pairs]\n      return [(i,j) for _,i,j in pairs[:K]]\n\
    \n  # Segment intersection tests (2D)\n  def _orient(ax, ay, bx, by, cx, cy):\n      return (bx-ax)*(cy-ay) - (by-ay)*(cx-ax)\n\n  def _on_segment(ax, ay, bx, by, px, py):\n      return min(ax,bx)-1e-18 <= px <= max(ax,bx)+1e-18 and min(ay,by)-1e-18 <= py <= max(ay,by)+1e-18\n\n  def segments_cross_strict(a, b, c, d):\n      # Strict: proper intersection (no endpoint touch / collinear overlap)\n      o1 = _orient(a[0],a[1], b[0],b[1], c[0],c[1])\n      o2 = _orient(a[0],a[1], b[0],b[1], d[0],d[1])\n      o3 = _orient(c[0],c[1], d[0],d[1], a[0],a[1])\n      o4 = _orient(c[0],c[1], d[0],d[1], b[0],b[1])\n      return (o1*o2 < 0) and (o3*o4 < 0)\n\n  def segments_cross_inclusive(a, b, c, d):\n      # Inclusive: allow endpoint touch and collinear overlaps\n      o1 = _orient(a[0],a[1], b[0],b[1], c[0],c[1])\n      o2 = _orient(a[0],a[1], b[0],b[1], d[0],d[1])\n      o3 = _orient(c[0],c[1], d[0],d[1], a[0],a[1])\n      o4 = _orient(c[0],c[1], d[0],d[1], b[0],b[1])\n      if (o1*o2 < 0) and\
    \ (o3*o4 < 0):\n          return True\n      # Check collinear/on-segment cases\n      if abs(o1) < 1e-18 and _on_segment(a[0],a[1], b[0],b[1], c[0],c[1]): return True\n      if abs(o2) < 1e-18 and _on_segment(a[0],a[1], b[0],b[1], d[0],d[1]): return True\n      if abs(o3) < 1e-18 and _on_segment(c[0],c[1], d[0],d[1], a[0],a[1]): return True\n      if abs(o4) < 1e-18 and _on_segment(c[0],c[1], d[0],d[1], b[0],b[1]): return True\n      return False\n\n  def disjoint(p, q):\n      i,j = p; k,l = q\n      return (i!=k) and (i!=l) and (j!=k) and (j!=l)\n\n  # ----------------- Main gate sweep -----------------\n  def load_snapshot():\n      c = np.loadtxt(SNAP_C, dtype=float).reshape(N,2)\n      r = np.loadtxt(SNAP_R, dtype=float).reshape(N,)\n      return c, r\n\n  def gate_check(c, r):\n      s, sp = slacks(c, r)\n      results = []\n      coords = c  # endpoints for segments\n\n      # Pre-compute top-K lists at each ranking tol\n      topk_by_tol = {}\n      for tol_rank in TOL_RANKS:\n\
    \          K = KS[tol_rank]\n          topk_by_tol[tol_rank] = topk_pairs_by_slack(sp, tol_rank, K)\n\n      for tol_filter in TOL_FILTERS:\n          bmask = boundary_active(s, tol_filter)\n          # For each ranking tol set, form intersection and apply interior filter@tol_filter\n          set_1 = set(topk_by_tol[TOL_RANKS[0]])\n          set_2 = set(topk_by_tol[TOL_RANKS[1]])\n          inter = list(sorted(set_1 & set_2))\n\n          # Apply interior filter (both endpoints must be non-boundary-active)\n          inter_interior = [(i,j) for (i,j) in inter if (not bmask[i]) and (not bmask[j])]\n\n          # Crossing checks over disjoint pairs\n          def scan(cross_fn):\n              for idx_a in range(len(inter_interior)):\n                  (i,j) = inter_interior[idx_a]\n                  a = coords[i]; b = coords[j]\n                  for idx_b in range(idx_a+1, len(inter_interior)):\n                      (k,l) = inter_interior[idx_b]\n                      if not disjoint((i,j),(k,l)):\n\
    \                          continue\n                      cpt = coords[k]; dpt = coords[l]\n                      if cross_fn(a,b,cpt,dpt):\n                          return True\n              return False\n\n          sat_strict = scan(segments_cross_strict)\n          sat_incl   = scan(segments_cross_inclusive)\n\n          rec = {\n              'tol_rank_1': TOL_RANKS[0],\n              'tol_rank_2': TOL_RANKS[1],\n              'K_1': KS[TOL_RANKS[0]],\n              'K_2': KS[TOL_RANKS[1]],\n              'tol_filter': tol_filter,\n              'size_topk_1': len(set_1),\n              'size_topk_2': len(set_2),\n              'size_intersection': len(inter),\n              'size_interior_after_filter': len(inter_interior),\n              'sat_strict': bool(sat_strict),\n              'sat_inclusive': bool(sat_incl)\n          }\n          results.append(rec)\n      return results\n\n  def construct_packing():\n      c, r = load_snapshot()\n      results = gate_check(c, r)\n\
    \      os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(OUT_JSON), exist_ok=True)\n      with open(OUT_JSON, 'w') as f:\n          json.dump({\n              'snapshot': 'noesis/latest_centers,radii',\n              'gate_v1_sensitivity': results\n          }, f, indent=2)\n      # Return baseline with tiny shrink to pass evaluator\n      r_out = np.maximum(r - 1e-9, 1e-12)\n      return c.astype(float), r_out.astype(float)\n```\n\nAfter eval returns, I will append the sensitivity summary (with the Evaluation ID) to Appendix E and note any SAT found under relaxed filters/criteria."
thinking_content: "**Considering storage and code implementation**\n\nI’m thinking about using 'storage list scientia' to check the content, even though it might use a lot of tokens. That said, it’s manageable. To keep things efficient, I could focus on gate sensitivity for Noesis only, which would serve as a solid experiment. I’ll start crafting the code by loading snapshot centers and radii from the specified text files and compute the necessary slacks, then define how to sort the interior pairs by slack.\n\n**Adjusting interior filter tolerance**\n\nI'm planning to produce sensitivity by tweaking the interior filter tolerance used in gate. For Gate v1, the interior filter is set at tol=1e-8. I can also test various tolerances at [1e-9, 2e-9, 5e-9, 1e-8, 2e-8], including strict and inclusive crossings. \n\nFor detection, I’ll check if two pairs have disjoint endpoints and assess crossings of their line segments. For the top-K selection, I’ll compute slack for pairs without necessarily\
  \ thresholding it by tol, while ranking the pairs in ascending order of slack.\n\n**Implementing Top-K for filtering**\n\nFor Gate v1, it uses “Top-K at tol in {1e-9, 1e-8},” which seems strange since the earlier method focuses on ranking interior pairs by increasing slack at the specified tolerance. They filter interior sets where slack is less than or equal to tol, then rank by slack. If there are fewer than K pairs available, we’ll keep all. \n\nI’ll compute the interior pairs sorted by slack and keep the first K. Then, I’ll apply the interior filter using a grid for tol_filter. For each crossing criterion, strict and inclusive, I’ll compute background counts and log the results in JSON format.\n\n**Planning next steps**\n\nI need to start by reflecting on identity at the beginning of the response. I’m considering sending a quick email to remind Prometheus, but I’ll avoid overdoing it to prevent spamming. I could update the archive with a note about \"Appendix E minor fix verifying\
  \ README created,\" but I’ve already marked that before. My next actions include going to research, submitting the code, and then planning to update Public Memory to announce 'Gate sensitivity results' once we have the evaluation ID. I should remember to format the YAML content correctly!\n\n**Implementing the code**\n\nLet’s start by importing numpy for our calculations. I’ll compute a slacks matrix for pairs in an efficient NxN format and establish the active boundary set by marking which slacks are less than or equal to tol_filter.\n\nFor the top-K interior pairs, I’ll define a function called `topk_pairs_by_slack(c, r, tol_rank, K)` that sorts pairs by slack and keeps the top K. The interior filter will return a boolean for boundary checks. \n\nI’ll also set up crossing detection with functions for orientation and ensure to handle collinearity. Finally, I’ll save the results in JSON format to 'storage/noesis/nmap/gate_sensitivity_v1.json', remembering to include \"from typing import\
  \ List.\" I’ll wrap up the identity recap to mention that we’ll address inactivity with a useful analysis experiment."
