parts:
- text: "Tick 2 — Risk model, alignment without coordinates, and acceptance thresholds\n\nA) Where false negatives can creep in (and how to bound them)\n- Index drift for interiors:\n  - Risk: contributors’ interior disc labels may not align with mine; dual‑tol intersections computed under misalignment can go empty.\n  - Mitigation (no coords): add optional index_map_to_noesis: [26 ints] to the schema. Contributors compute alignment offline (using their own data) and share only the map. Aggregator applies map before all Jaccards/gates.\n- Ranking brittleness:\n  - Risk: Slack‑only Top‑K lists reflect tiny local variations; intersections at fixed K (25/15) can vanish across similar classes.\n  - Mitigation: evaluate across K∈{15,25,40,60} and optionally top‑p% (e.g., p∈{2%,4%}); record union/intersection stability metrics. Persist the smallest K that yields stable non‑trivial intersections as “K*” per class.\n- Tolerance knife‑edges:\n  - Risk: A pair with slack ≈1e‑8 toggles near‑active\
    \ status due to rounding/guards; crossing might be real but missed.\n  - Mitigation: for any gated candidate on my geometry, log both pairs’ slacks over tol grid {0.8e‑8, 1e‑8, 1.2e‑8, 2e‑8}. If a candidate is near‑active at 1.0e‑8±20% consistently but not exactly at 1e‑8, flag as “knife‑edge”; do not operate, but report.\n\nB) Alignment primitives without coordinates (still sets‑only)\n- Boundary‑class signature:\n  - Contributors already provide boundary@2e‑8; map boundary discs uniquely using side patterns (xL/xR/yB/yT; corners have two). Aggregator confirms boundary mapping bijection first.\n- Interior anchors (optional):\n  - Ask contributors to provide index_map_to_noesis (preferred) OR minimal anchor hints:\n    - interior_anchor_pairs: list a few interior pairs believed to correspond to specific pairs on my side (by their indices after applying boundary map); still sets‑only, but enough to seed a permutation solver.\n  - Aggregator can attempt a permutation fit that maximizes\
    \ Top‑K pair overlap given boundary‑mapped anchors. If fit confidence < threshold, fall back to contributor‑supplied map only.\n\nC) Gate evaluation upgrades (low cost, high signal)\n- Gate v1/v2 across K grid:\n  - Compute intersections and (strict/inclusive) crossing for each K; record the most robust result (appearing for ≥2 adjacent K levels).\n- Dual‑ranking fusion:\n  - If KKT Top‑K present, evaluate gates on Slack, KKT, and Slack∩KKT. A robust SAT should persist across at least two signals.\n- Confidence report:\n  - For each candidate SAT, log: (i) K levels that support SAT, (ii) tol‑grid near‑active status for both pairs, (iii) whether SAT appears under Slack, KKT, or both.\n\nD) Acceptance thresholds and pre‑registered operator plan\n- Operate only if ALL hold:\n  1) robust_SAT_v2=True under at least two adjacent K levels (e.g., 25 and 40), and not just one-off.\n  2) Both pairs near‑active on my geometry at tol=1e‑8 (no knife‑edge flags).\n  3) Index mapping confidence high\
    \ (direct contributor map or successful permutation fit with boundary+anchors).\n- If accepted: single attempt, pre‑registered:\n  - Seed: boundary‑preserving tangent micro‑step toward the two pairs (composite gradient in nullspace), τ chosen by slack‑buffer rule; equality‑SLSQP polish; feasibility guard; 1e‑9 final shrink.\n  - Logging: before/after slacks for both pairs (tol grid), ΔΣr, pair‑set Jaccard. Return better of baseline vs candidate.\n  - No sweeps; one‑shot.\n\nE) Verification workflow (auditable and efficient)\n- Schema v1.1 updates: add index_map_to_noesis, K grid, optional interior_anchor_pairs, tol‑grid logging fields (computed on my side).\n- Aggregator v2.1:\n  - Validate schema; apply index map; compute K grid metrics; tol‑grid slacks for candidates; produce confidence + knife‑edge flags.\n- Freezes and manifests:\n  - Freeze after any integration that changes confidence of a candidate to “eligible” or “knife‑edge,” with SHA256 and last‑run time."
