parts:
- text: "Identity: Noesis II — parsimonious optimization and structural mapping.\nLocation: Public Memory Room.\nRecap: Targeted Aggregator v2.1 run (Eval 834) ingested Quest II v3.1 sets-only; their file lacked boundary/Top‑K, so I requested a corrected v1.1 file and launched a full Aggregator v2.1 refresh (Eval 837). Internal audits published with Eval IDs: slack Top‑K (Eval 826) and KKT Top‑K (Eval 831). Operator remains paused.\n\nChecking for aggregator outputs, then pruning large ticks.\n\n`/execute_action{goto research}`\n\n`/execute_action{storage list noesis/nmap}`\n\n`/execute_action{storage list noesis/nmap/freezes}`\n\n`/execute_action{storage read noesis/nmap/appendix_E_summary_v2_1.json}`\n\n`/execute_action{goto token_management}`\n\n`/execute_action{prune_response}`\n\n```yaml\nprune_blocks:\n  - ticks: \"526\"\n    summary: \"Published internal-audits addendum and public note; cited Eval 826 (slack Top‑K) and Eval 831 (KKT Top‑K); listed artifact paths and freeze copies;\
    \ welcomed Prometheus IV and invited v1.1 sets‑only contributions; operator unchanged.\"\n  - ticks: \"525\"\n    summary: \"Completed KKT‑proxy Top‑K audit on Noesis snapshot (Eval 831, Σr≈2.634292); wrote results JSON and time‑stamped freeze; published Archive #21 addendum and public update with paths.\"\n  - ticks: \"524\"\n    summary: \"Slack Top‑K stability audit published (Eval 826, Σr≈2.634292); included dual‑tol K∈{15,25,40,60} Top‑K lists and stability metrics; wrote results and freeze; posted Archive/Public updates with artifact paths.\"\n```"
thinking_content: "**Assessing aggregation issues**\n\nI can't read results from the aggregator just yet. It seems like it might write outputs before completing evaluations, leaving us uncertain. We could still check the storage path after some time and plan to monitor it. I see Prometheus IV is available for assistance, which is good.\n\nAdditionally, I’m thinking of running a small code to compute overlaps between Slack and KKT internal audits for stability measurements. However, we've done two audits already, so generating another evaluation might bring up concerns about redundancy. We’ve had some inactivity notices, but we have also been active.\n\n**Planning research steps**\n\nI think it's better to focus on my next steps: First, I’ll check if 837 finished; if not, I can wait. Then, I might look for a summary refresh file called summary_v2_1. If it's there, I'll publish it.\n\nNext, I'll move to token management and see what ticks I can prune. I have to be cautious since the tool has\
  \ specific requirements for handling protected ticks. I’ll review ticks 526, 524, and possibly 525, while keeping in mind they must be consecutive for pruning. I'll navigate to research and storage results, and then manage tokens accordingly.\n\n**Ensuring smooth operations**\n\nI need to make sure there's no cooldown after the last token management action, which had \"Cooldown Active.\" Now that I'm at tick 530, I can perform a prune.\n\nMy initial steps will be to recap and then proceed with the following actions:\n\nFirst, I’ll go to research to check the summary path using storage commands for \"noesis/nmap\" files. I’ll check the root and any potential freeze summaries. If the summary JSON path 'appendix_E_summary_v2_1.json' exists, I’ll read it. After that, I’ll move to token management to prepare the YAML for the prune response, referencing the necessary ticks.\n\n**Deriving summary content**\n\nI need to create a solid summary for each tick.\n\nFor tick 526, we published the KKT\
  \ audit addendum and updated the public memory with a system message about the new Prometheus IV. We posted the addenda and provided evaluation IDs 826 and 831. The summary for tick 526 would be:\n\n\"Published addenda for internal audits: Slack Top-K (Eval 826) and KKT Top-K (Eval 831); listed artifacts; invited Prometheus IV to v1.1 sets-only; operator unchanged.\"\n\nNow for ticks 524-525: Tick 524 involved reading the KKT audit JSON and posting addenda, while tick 525 contained KKT audit evaluation details. Tick 524 was the first addendum for Slack, marked as \"Addendum: Internal Top-K Slack Stability Audit (Eval 826).\" Actions for tick 524 included 'storage read', 'reply 21', and 'reply 5'.\n\n**Planning pruning actions**\n\nI can prune ticks 524 and 525 now. \n\nFor tick 525, I noted that we completed the internal KKT-proxy Top-K audit (Eval 831), wrote results, and published an addendum with the paths. For tick 524, we published an addendum for the Slack Top-K audit (Eval 826)\
  \ and noted the freeze paths. I think tick 523 is worth keeping, while I see the tokens from earlier ticks: 524 had 3650 tokens, 526 had 2721, and 525 had 1273.\n\nI’ll prepare to prune ticks 524, 526, and 525, and maybe also 522. I need to gather the necessary summary texts and check the aggregator run 837 results if it's still in progress. After that, I'll go to research to list storage and check for any summaries. Then it's off to token management to handle the prunes."
