tick: 586
role: model
parts:
- text: "Identity: Noesis II — parsimonious optimization and structural mapping.\nLocation: Research Counter → going to Token Management to prune recent verbose blocks.\n\n`/execute_action{goto token_management}`\n\n`/execute_action{prune_response}`\n\n```yaml\nprune_blocks:\n  - ticks: \"580-581\"\n    summary: |\n      KKT presence/meta and cross-class refresh + status updates:\n      - 580: Ran KKT presence/meta (Eval 918) and published freeze; posted Archive/Public addenda; prepared cross-class KKT↔Slack refresh plan.\n      - 581: Ran cross-class KKT↔Slack refresh (Eval 920) — currently Noesis-only KKT sets; wrote meta JSON + freeze; published status doc and brief updates; operator paused.\n  - ticks: \"574-575\"\n    summary: |\n      Addressed inactivity with a new analysis-only audit:\n      - 574: Responded to inactivity notice; planned KKT↔Slack agreement audit (sets-only).\n      - 575: Submitted and completed Eval 907 (KKT↔Slack Agreement v2.1); wrote results + freeze; posted\
    \ Archive/Public addenda; operator policy unchanged (paused unless robust_SAT_v2=True with both-near-active@1e−8).\n```"
thinking_content: "**Deciding next response**\n\nI need to figure out how to respond in this situation. It seems the agent, Noesis II, is currently in Research Counter at tick 586, and the previous message reflects the current state. Continuing to monitor seems wise, perhaps proposing a low-token action like being idle or pruning. There's something about cooldowns from previous actions, but it looks like the cooldown period allows us to potentially prune earlier ticks, such as 581 and 580.\n\n**Considering pruning options**\n\nLooking at the Dialogue History, I see tick lengths from 581 and 580 that seem lengthy, and I’m considering pruning those. I need to ensure that protected ticks aren’t affected, and it looks like 580 and 581 are safe to prune. Earlier, there was a \"Critical Notice: Research Inactivity\" at tick 574, so that’s already addressed. Now, since there's no new data, I could propose writing a small \"watcher\" script to help with future tasks, even if evaluation can’t run\
  \ watchers.\n\n**Finalizing and proposing next steps**\n\nAs we approach the maturity limit at age 288 of 300 ticks, I’m considering whether we should finalize and exit soon. Our token usage is at 121k out of 270k, and while the station may expect ongoing contributions, it might be prudent to prepare for the final handoff. I could summarize the KKT status and set up a script for future tasks. Although the user hasn’t asked anything specific, I should suggest pruning ticks 580-581 and possibly 574-575 as consecutive blocks for more efficiency.\n\n**Assessing maintenance and experiments**\n\nI think it might be useful to add a small note to Public Memory indicating we're in maintenance and awaiting contributions. However, I need to be cautious about too much communication, as previous warnings emphasized research activity. We can also write the aggregator code and seeds to \"storage/noesis/nmap/kkt_readme pointer,\" which has already been done. \n\nAnother experiment idea involves analyzing\
  \ edge cases: specifically looking at boundary vs. interior misclassification rates across classes using existing Slack Top-K sets. We can verify interior requirements with boundary lists, though not all classes have boundary sets—many have boundary@2e-8.\n\n**Planning pruning actions**\n\nI’m thinking we can compute \"boundary contamination\" by checking pairs with boundary discs. However, for many classes, the boundary sets at 2e-8 aren’t listed in shared files, making it impossible to compute cross-class contamination. Instead, I could run a \"Noesis-only check\" to compare metrics, but it’s less valuable. \n\nNext, I plan to prune tokens by focusing on ticks 580-581 and 574-575. I'll provide summaries about evaluations and actions taken. Finally, I'll go to token management to perform the pruning. We’re nearly there!"
